{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/about-us/civil-works-estates", "content": "Directorate of Civil Works & Estates (DCW&E) is the nodal agency for infrastructure related requirements of DRDO to take care of estate and maintenance of DRDO assets. This Directorate delivering infrastructure, desired by the users, at desired place, in the desired time frame, with desired quality. DCW&E also caters to the infrastructure requirements of Army, Navy, Air Force, NTRO, SFC, ATVP, and other Government Ministries."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/about-us/directorate-futuristic-technology-management-dftm", "content": "Directorate of Futuristic Technology Management (DFTM) identifies the emerging and futuristic technologies required for defence and lays down the Long Term Research roadmap for these technologies. To enable the directed research, eco-system a Long Term Directed Research Policy (LTDRP) has been formulated for long term engagement of academia, research institutes and startups/industries. The policy has a collaborative framework for planning and implementing directed research for developing futuristic defence technologies by bringing\ntogether all stake-holders.\nDFTM undertakes the following major activities to plan, implement and manage the directed research collaboratively:\nIdentify crucial & futuristic technologies and associated technology gaps.\nWork out research problems and definitions with broad technical specifications related to identified technologies/technology gaps for collaborative directed R&D\nEstablish DRDO Industry Academia Centres of Excellence (DIA-CoE)\nIdentify and establish the state-of-the-art research facilities at DIA-CoEs\nEngage startups / industries for innovative technology development at DIA-CoEs\nFacilitate international research collaboration through DIA-CoEs\nCoordinate with Scientific & Research organisations for resources at national level\nEnter into contract with identified agencies/consortiums and coordinate for achieving the desired objectives\nDRDO has established 15 DIA-CoEs under the LTDRP. The DFTM directorate at DRDO HQ sets the policies, standard operating procedures and guidelines for the centre operations. The directorate oversees the project review and sanction process, in addition to the long term roadmap for the futuristic technologies.\nFifteen DIA-CoEs have been established at identified academic Institutes and Universities in the country to build a directed research eco-system. These centres enable the collaboration\namong DRDO labs, academia and startups/industries. Under the projects through DIA-CoEs research is being pursued in emerging as well as advanced technologies.\nKey features of DIA -CoE projects\nThese centres enable the hub and spoke model of research. The projects from any academic institute under the verticals of the centre can be considered and will be governed by the respective centre.\nThe scheme enables Co-PIs from multiple institutes to enable true collaboration in the country.\nThrough internship and hackathons, centres aim to actively involve students to solve specific problems of challenging nature"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/about-us/directorate-low-intensity-conflict-dlic", "content": "Directorate of Low Intensity Conflict (DLIC) supports and assists DRDO laboratories in developing technology solutions for the Armed Forces, Paramilitary Forces and the Police Forces to combat LIC operations effectively. DLIC helps MHA, Central Police Forces and State Police Forces in formulation of their medium term and long term modernization plans. It also advises the concerned organizations (including field level units) regarding operational aspects and SOPs for effective and optimal utilization (including training) of products and systems, developed by DRDO; on innovative and effective utilization of the available resources in specific areas/regions and specific situations; and supports the concerned organizations in induction of new products, equipment and systems (including product specification, arranging demonstrations, conduct of trials, etc). It also helps industry/development partners in realization of modified prototypes of DRDO developed products and systems to meet the specific need of the users and interacts with academic institutions at National and International level and other agencies for seeding of new technologies relevant to LIC."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/about-us/directorate-parliamentary-affairs-dpa", "content": "Directorate of Parliamentary Affairs, Rajbhasha & Organisation and Methods (DPARO&M) deals with parliament matters concerning Department of Defence R&D (DDR&D). It helps Parliamentary Committees in all matters related to DDR&D and coordinates their visits to establishments under DDR&D. Directorate of Rajbhasha and O&M implements Official Language Policy of Rajbhasha Department, Ministry of Home Affairs. The aim of this Directorate is to achieve goals under the guidelines of Department of Official Language (OL), Ministry of Defence and the Committee of Parliament on OL by implementing Official Language Policy in Headquarters and Laboratories/Establishments/Centres of DDR&D.\nरक्षा अनुसंधान भारती  वर्ष 2023 | 17वाँ अंक\nरक्षा अनुसंधान भारती  अक्तूबर 2023 -मार्च 2024 । 18वाँ अंक  (जैव विज्ञान तकनीकी विशेषांक)\nसुविज्ञ अंक - 19, वर्ष 2023\nसुविज्ञ तकनीकी अंक - 3, वर्ष 2023\n,\nप्रतिबिम्ब (साहित्यिक खंड) अंक 15, वर्ष 2023-2024\nप्रतिबिम्ब (तकनीकी खंड) अंक 2, वर्ष 2023-2024\nसंकल्प अंक - 35, वर्ष 2023-24\nसंकल्प तकनीकी पत्रिका अंक-03, वर्ष 2023-24\nउडान तकनीकी, अंक-01, वर्ष 2023-2024\nउडान, अंक-20, वर्ष 2023-2024\nविश्लेषिका, अंक 23 वर्ष 2023-24 (तकनीकी एवं वैज्ञानिक लेखन)\nविश्लेषिका, अंक 23 वर्ष 2023-24 (साहित्यिक एवं विविध)\nरक्षा अनुसंधान भारती, विकसित भारत अभियान 2047, अप्रैल-सितंबर 2024, 19वां अंक\nरक्षा अनुसंधान भारती, राजभाषा हीरक जयंती वर्ष , अक्टूबर 2024- मार्च 2025, 20वां अंक"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/about-us/directorate-quality-reliability-safety-dqrs", "content": "Directorate of Quality Reliability and Safety was created at DRDO Headquarters, New Delhi on 27 Sep 2012. To achieve its mission, Directorate organises events and conducts trainings on quality, reliability and safety aspects and works towards embedding the same in the organisational culture. Being the nodal agency on quality and reliability, Directorate keeps live interfaces with external agencies on the subject. DQR&S issues policies, guidelines and standards so that DRDO products/technologies are of highest quality, reliable and safe for the end user. DQR&S facilitates Labs/Estts in building quality from requirements stage right through development, prototype and production. The Directorate ensures that Labs/Estts build the highest quality Software by following the latest standards under a uniform development, SQA and IV&V framework."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/about-us/dte-interaction-services-business-disb", "content": "The Directorate is the nodal point of interaction with Services for all matters and interfaces with the Services and the DRDO Labs for identification of systems to be developed by DRDO, induction of DRDO developed systems and provide technology solutions for User requirements. This Directorate is also responsible for formulation of DRDO’s views on policies related to acquisition of systems by the Armed Forces.\nAbout Directorate\nDirectorate of Interaction with Services for Business (DISB) is a Corporate Directorate created under CC R&D (SI) in Nov 2005. The Directorate is headed by a Scientist ‘G’ (Director) and has a mix of Scientists and Service Officers under its fold. The Service Officers are Subject Matter Experts (SME) posted to DISB as an interface between Services and DRDO Labs. DISB coordinates all activities related to long term planning, identification of projects to be taken up, formulation of Services Qualitative Requirements (SQR), conduct of periodic reviews, conduct of trials etc. and functions in a systematic way persevering with various stakeholders to ensure induction of indigenously developed systems for equipping of the Indian Armed Forces with indigenous solutions. The Directorate is the fulcrum of all Services’ related activities of a project’s journey from proverbial “womb to tomb”.\nVision\nTo provide the Armed Forces with fighting superiority and decisive edge over enemy by equipping them with indigenously designed, developed and manufactured state-of-the-art weapon systems and platforms.\nMission\nIdentification of technologies/systems to be developed to meet the futuristic requirements of the Armed Forces\nFormulation of policies for enhancing self-reliance in defence\nSupport design, development and induction of indigenous systems by synergising DRDO labs, Users, Production Agencies and other stakeholders"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/about-us/extramural-research-intellectual-property-rights-er-ipr#top", "content": "Directorate of Extranural Research & Intellectual Property Right (DER&IPR) works towards establishing network with research community in Indian academic research centres, creation of centres of excellence within academic institutes, augmentation of research infrastructures and supporting advanced research through projects. The aim of ER&IPR directorate if to develop essential knowledge and capabilities for DRDO’s immediate and futuristic needs for defence product development and technologies."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/about-us/finance-and-materials-management", "content": "Directorate of Finance and Materials Management (DFMM) is responsible formulation of policies and their implementation for materials procurement and materials management, budgetary estimates, allocation of funds and nodal agency for Audit related matters."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/about-us/human-resource-development#top", "content": "Directorate of Human Resource Development (HRD) is responsible for planning and management of human resource in DRDO. It formulates policies regarding recruitment, career progression and quality improvement of manpower. DHRD also provides manpower and vehicle authorization to DRDO laboratories and establishment through systematic planning. DHRD helps employees in developing their personal and organization skills, knowledge and abilities. The focus is to evolve the most superior workforce so that the organization and individual employees can accomplish their goals."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/about-us/industry-interface-technology-management#top", "content": "Introduction\nDirectorate of Industry Interface & Technology Management (DIITM) became functional on 07 Dec 2009. This was formed by merger of C-Tech and Directorate of Technology Acquisition. DIITM serves as a Single Window in DRDO for administering Policies and matters related to Industry Interface and Technology Management.\nDIITM plays a vital role in filling gap between Defence R&D and production by facilitating Transfer of Technologies (ToT) to industries. DIITM facilitates Limited Series Production (LSP) to cater the need of production of limited quantity of DRDO developed products. It also coordinates the production with industries to produce DRDO developed products as per Users’ requirements. DIITM is nodal directorate for the technology acquisition by DRDO under defence offsets. DIITM provides necessary policy framework and coordination for providing DRDO Test Facilities to the industries. DIITM also provides handholding support to the industries for the export of DRDO developed products.\nThe major achievements of DIITM are given below:-\nFramed the “DRDO Policy for Transfer of Technology” and promulgated after approval of Hon’ble Raksha Mantri.\nFormulated the ‘DRDO Procedures for Transfer of Technology’ with the approval of Secretary DD R&D and chairman DRDO to implement the new ToT Policy.\nMore than 1600 Licensing agreements for ToTs have been signed with the Industries including major weapon systems like Fiber optics Gyro, Varunastra, Medium Power Radar etc.\nCommercialisation of DRDO developed technologies have been done for the benefit of society at large through transfer of dual use (military and civil) spin-off technologies to industries.\nA mass awareness programme is continuing for the awareness of industries on DRDO developed technologies.\nLimited Series Production of various DRDO products has been carried out for Indian Armed Forces.\nDRDO has processed more than 700 NOC requests for exports.\nNecessary handholding for specification and response to EOI/ RFI for export was provided to industries for export.\nIndustry compendium containing details of industry partners who have partnered with DRDO at various stages has been compiled by DIITM.\nPrepared compendium of products which have huge export potential.\nGuidelines for availing DRDO Test facilities by industries have been formulated.\nFormulated SOP for export.\nGuidelines for production coordination."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/about-us/information-technology-and-cyber-security", "content": "Directorate of Information Technology and Cyber Security (DIT&CS) looks after Information Technology and Cyber security related activities in DRDO. DIT&CS operates and maintains DRDO Organizational Network and Centralized Internet Access Gateway for all labs and establishments of DRDO. The directorate develops new software application for DRDO HQ and labs, conducts IT security Audit of all labs/estts, frames cyber security related policies for DRDO and develops cyber security solutions."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/about-us/international-cooperation", "content": "The International Cooperation activities in DRDO are coordinated by the Directorate of International Cooperation (DIC). The Directorate provides a single window to DRDO labs for all activities concerning International Cooperation. DIC guides, prioritizes and coordinates international cooperation in consonance with the vision, mission and the technology road map of DRDO. Recognising the importance of international defence R&D cooperation extend technology progress and opportunities to develop platforms, weapons, sensors and systems DIC’s endeavour aims at: Sharing, partnering and accessing the best defence technology available worldwide; reducing cost, effort and time; compressing technology capability gaps; strengthening relations with friendly countries; and presenting DRDO as technological strength for leveraging home grown technology through a joint foreign partnership.\nDRDO pursues Defence Technology Cooperation with friendly foreign countries through the G2G Arrangement/ Framework. Proposals relating to Defence Technology Cooperation with foreign partners in consonance with the vision and mission of DRDO are welcome from individuals/ Academia/ Industry. Contact at\nemail:\nintcoop[at]hqr[dot]drdo[dot]in"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/about-us/management-services", "content": "Directorate of Management Services (DMS) is responsible for management of DRDO Bhawan operations and administration including matters, like general administration, procurement, contingency, telephones, travel and other logistics."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/about-us/personnel#top", "content": "The Directorate of Personnel (DoP) at DRDO HQ is one of the Directorates of the HR cluster, which is responsible for Personnel Management, Services and Administrative matters of DRDO employees. DoP formulates policies on all matters of personnel/services/administration and maintains the integrity of public services.\nAbout Directorate\nDirectorate of Personnel, as one of the important corporate directorates, plays a vital role in implementing Government of India/DRDO policies in the areas of establishment/administration and looks after the personnel & service matters of all officials working in the Laboratories/Establishments/Directorates of HQr and Programme/Projects of DRDO. In its larger role, it acts as the implementer of policies and ensures that accepted standards and norms of public administration, as laid down by Government of India, are followed in the recruitment, regulation of service conditions, posting/transfers, deputation of personnel and all other related issues.\nDoP advises DRDO Labs/Estts on issues of personnel management and in matters such as service and conduct rules. DoP frames rules, SOPs and issues amendments in keeping with the changing times to bring about efficient administration. DoP is also responsible for supervision, monitoring and review of work related to enforcing accountability. Apart from efforts to improve efficiency and accountability, the directorate also looks after welfare of employees, Joint Consultative Machinery (JCM), reservation for SC/ST/OBC, Persons With Disabilities (PWD) (Divyangjan) and ex-servicemen.\nDoP also deals with cases of appointment to the posts of Director Generals (DGs), Directors, DRDO Chairs, Fellowships etc. It is also the nodal agency for implementation of GoI e-governance programs viz. AVMS, AEBAS, CPGRAMS, LIMBS and ANUBHAV.\nDirectorate of Personnel is the first ISO 9001:2000 certified Directorate at DRDO HQr. The Directorate has been recertified with ISO 9001:2008 for carrying out its functions efficiently, effectively and timely.\nHistorical Background\nDirectorate of Personnel (DoP) is one of the most important corporate Directorates entrusted with implementation of policies and guidelines pertaining to personnel matters disseminated by DRDO, DOP&T and other Govt of India agencies. Its activities include administrative and personnel matters of DRDO employees. Directorate of Personnel was carved out of Directorate of Administration in late 1960s and was formed in 1972."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/about-us/planning-coordination", "content": "Directorate of Planning & Coordination (DP&C) is the nodal directorate of DRDO for interface with other Ministries and Departments and for coordination within DRDO. DP&C evolves guidelines for project formulation, management and all other project related aspects and ensures their uniform implementation in DRDO. The Directorate maintains up-to-date information of all DRDO projects, compiles progress of major projects, processes high value project proposals for approval, publishes DRDO Annual Report and coordinates with MoD for their Annual Report. It functions as apex board Secretariat for high value and critical Project and Programmes. DP&C acts as a nodal point for Long Term Technology Perspective Plan (LTTPP), plan, vision and roadmap for the organization and its revisions. It establishes corporate review policies.\nThe Directorate liaises with all Ministries & Department of GOI for providing inputs and feedback and evolves and conveys organization’s response to draft policies, legislations and guidelines, etc. issued by other GOI Ministries and Departments. DP&C also deals with matter related to allocation of Business Rules and Citizen Charter of DDR&D."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/about-us/public-interface", "content": "Directorate of Public Interface (DPI) is the nodal point to drive all media campaigns, public outreach activities and interactions facilitated through press releases, events/product launches/exhibitions/seminars, etc. DPI organizes one-to-one interactions/interviews, press conferences, coordinates DRDO participation in Republic Day Parade, production of Digital contents for promotional activities of DRDO (movies, teasers, animations, etc.), publishing of DRDO advertisements in magazines & newspapers, creative designing of brochures, leaflets, hoardings, posters, conducting of National and International Exhibitions, liasioning with Directorate of Public Relations (DPR), Ministry of Defence and other agencies, etc."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/aboutus/directorate-futuristic-technology-management-dftm#top", "content": "Directorate of Futuristic Technology Management (DFTM) identifies the emerging and futuristic technologies required for defence and lays down the Long Term Research roadmap for these technologies. To enable the directed research, eco-system a Long Term Directed Research Policy (LTDRP) has been formulated for long term engagement of academia, research institutes and startups/industries. The policy has a collaborative framework for planning and implementing directed research for developing futuristic defence technologies by bringing\ntogether all stake-holders.\nDFTM undertakes the following major activities to plan, implement and manage the directed research collaboratively:\nIdentify crucial & futuristic technologies and associated technology gaps.\nWork out research problems and definitions with broad technical specifications related to identified technologies/technology gaps for collaborative directed R&D\nEstablish DRDO Industry Academia Centres of Excellence (DIA-CoE)\nIdentify and establish the state-of-the-art research facilities at DIA-CoEs\nEngage startups / industries for innovative technology development at DIA-CoEs\nFacilitate international research collaboration through DIA-CoEs\nCoordinate with Scientific & Research organisations for resources at national level\nEnter into contract with identified agencies/consortiums and coordinate for achieving the desired objectives\nDRDO has established 15 DIA-CoEs under the LTDRP. The DFTM directorate at DRDO HQ sets the policies, standard operating procedures and guidelines for the centre operations. The directorate oversees the project review and sanction process, in addition to the long term roadmap for the futuristic technologies.\nFifteen DIA-CoEs have been established at identified academic Institutes and Universities in the country to build a directed research eco-system. These centres enable the collaboration\namong DRDO labs, academia and startups/industries. Under the projects through DIA-CoEs research is being pursued in emerging as well as advanced technologies.\nKey features of DIA -CoE projects\nThese centres enable the hub and spoke model of research. The projects from any academic institute under the verticals of the centre can be considered and will be governed by the respective centre.\nThe scheme enables Co-PIs from multiple institutes to enable true collaboration in the country.\nThrough internship and hackathons, centres aim to actively involve students to solve specific problems of challenging nature"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/area-of-work/civil-works-estates", "content": "Responsibilities\nProposal from Labs / Establishments.\nSecurity & Acceptance of Necessity.\nConvening / attending Boards.\nProcessing works for sanction.\nExecution of works.\nMonitoring progress.\nBudget allocation and control.\nMaintenance Support.\nLand acquisition.\nPolicy formulation.\nQuality assurance.\nAudit Paras, Loss Statement etc.\nMaintenance of Database.\nType of Works Undertaken\nCapital Works (4076 Head of A/c)\nAll New Works\nBuildings\nSecurity\nInfrastructure\nSpecial Items\nRevenue Works (2080 Head of A/c)\nAll Maintenance Repairs\nRepairs to Facilities\nRent and Allied Charges\nRunning of Guest Houses\nWatch & Ward / Conservancy\nMinor Works\nEstablishments\nDte of Civil Works & Estates\nChief Engineer (R&D) Delhi\nChief Engineer (R&D) Secunderabad\nChief Construction Engineer (R&D) North\nChief Construction Engineer (R&D) Central\nChief Construction Engineer (R&D) South\nChief Construction Engineer (R&D) West\nChief Construction Engineer (R&D) East\nChief Construction Engineer (R&D) Estates\nChief Construction Engineer (R&D) Delhi\nEstate Management Units (12 Units)\nEstate Management Support & Land Acquisition Functions\nEstate Management Support\nMaintenance and upkeep of DRDO Estates.\nAdministrative control.\nArboriculture.\nAllotment of residential and hostel accommodation.\nSecurity management of the Estates.\nConservancy services in residential Estates.\nManagement of Transit Facilities and Guest Houses.\nManagement of various facilities like Schools for Children, Cinema Halls, Sports and Ground Facilities, land acquisition.\nProjection of requirement.\nJustification.\nIdentification of land.\nCollection of revenue details and costing.\nStatement of Case.\nLand acquisition through DGDE.\nPublication of Notification.\nGeneral User Information\nEnlistment of Estate Management and Maintenance Contractors(DRDO) Rules- 2015\n420 KB\nApplication for Pre-Qualification and Purchase of Tender for Construction of \"Rajaswa Bhawan\" Tax HQ Building, at Kasturba Ghandi Marg, New Delhi\n461 KB\nDetails of Beneficiaries of Shopping Complex\nTimarpur, Delhi under EMU (R & D) Delhi\n602 KB\nJodhpur under EMU (R & D)\nAvadi, Chennai under EMU (R & D)\nTStatement Showing the information on the Commercial Exploitation of Defence Land with reference to Starred Question No. 192 of Rajya Sabha (1)\n98 KB\nStatement Showing the information on the Commercial Exploitation of Defence Land with reference to Starred Question No. 192 of Rajya Sabha (2)\n89 KB\nStatement Showing the information on the Commercial Exploitation of Defence Land with reference to Starred Question No. 192 of Rajya Sabha (3)\n40 KB"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/area-of-work/directorate-low-intensity-conflict-dlic", "content": "Support to CAPFs / State Police Forces in their modernization plans through identifying DRDO developed products suitable for Low Intensity Conflicts (LIC) / Homeland Security and proposing the same to MHA / CAPFs / State Police Forces on receipt of their requirements.\nSteer customization of DRDO products as per user requirements, coordinate lab demonstrations and field trials for product improvements in spiral mode\nCoordinate with the DRDO labs to develop robust technologies and systems for emergent new requirements of CAPFs/State Police Forces to combat LIC operations effectively.\nExposing MHA / CAPFs / State Police Forces to DRDO developed technologies and systems having potential applications for LIC and Homeland Security.\nInteract with DRDO labs for innovative and effective utilization of the developed technologies for Low Intensity Conflicts and Homeland Security.\nCoordinate with MHA and users for induction of DRDO developed products found suitable by them.\nShowcasing DRDO developed technologies and systems to CAPFs / State Police Forces through participation in Seminars, Exhibitions and Conferences."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/area-of-work/directorate-parliamentary-affairs-dpa", "content": "For matters/issues pertaining to Dept. of Defence R&D, this Directorate acts as a Nodal agency for:\nInteraction with D(Parl)/Department of Ministry of Defence (MoD) and other Ministries for Parliament related issues.\nAnalysis of Parliament Questions received through D(Parl)/MoD for transfer or acceptance or for disallowance, if necessary.\nPreparing Draft Replies to Parliament Questions during Budget, Monsoon and Winter Sessions.\nProcessing case for Fulfillment/Dropping/Extension of Parliament Assurances.\nCoordinating Study visit of Parliament Standing Committee of Defence to DRDO Labs/Estt.\nMatters related to Parliamentary Committee(s) and other Departmental related Standing Committee(s).\nMatters related to Consultative Committee for the Ministry of Defence.\nLiaison with various wings of Ministry of Defence and other Ministries/Departments related to Parliamentary Affairs.\nTo ensure implementation of Official Language Policy of Government of India in DRDO\nTo co-ordinate and conduct meetings of Official Language implementation Committee of DRDO HQ\nCompilation of various Organisation Manual, Repository of various orders related to Organisation.\nRecords Management, File Management and scrutiny of relevant reports.\nImplementation of Central Secretariat Manual of Office Procedure (CSMOP) in DRDO.\nDirectorate of Rajbhasha and O&M works in the following areas\nImplementation and monitoring of the Official Language Policy and rules of Union in DRDO HQ & its Laboratories/Establishments/Centres, circulate the Presidential Orders regarding the Official Language, general orders, executive instructions/directions issued from time to time & other regulatory directions and the Annual Programme, issue guidelines to achieve the targets as mentioned in the Annual Programme.\nConducting regularly quarterly Official Language Implementation Committee (OLIC) meetings.\nUndertaking Hindi translation work from English to Hindi and vice versa.\nOrganizing Hindi workshop (minimum four workshops in a year) on Rajbhasha related topics, Hindi fortnight and Hindi Diwas.\nCoordination and support to DRDO Laboratories/Establishments/Centres for conducting Official Language Inspection by the Committee of Parliament on Official Language.\nAssistance and participation in Joint Official Language Inspection (DRDO and MOD) of various Laboratories/Establishments/Centres of DRDO.\nConducting Headquarter level Official Language Inspection of various at least 25% Directorates/Laboratories/Establishments/Centres of DRDO for progressive use of Hindi.\nImplementation of all Hindi Incentive Schemes, Hindi dictation Schemes, Rajbhasha Gaurav and Rajbhasha Kirti Award Schemes etc promulgated by Ministry of Home Affairs and Book Award Scheme of DRDO for progressive use of Hindi in various DRDO HQ Directorates/Laboratories/Establishments/Centres.\nObtain budget forecast from all the Laboratories/Establishments/Centres of DRDO for the activities related to implementation of Official Language and compile the same and project to DFMM, DRDO HQ leading to consolidated Rajbhasha budget allocation for DRDO Laboratories/Establishments/Centres.\nReview of quarterly progress reports on Official Language work of DRDO HQ and Laboratories/Establishments/Centres and send review report for implementation.\nActive Participation in quarterly Official Language Implementation Committee (OLIC) meeting of MOD Chaired by JS & CAO & follow up actions.\nAssistance/Guidance to various Laboratories/Establishments/Centres of DRDO for various Rajbhasha activities including organization of Seminar/Symposium.\nTranslation and refresher Training of Translators/Assistant Director (OL)/Deputy Director(OL)/Joint Director (OL) and nominating personnel for Hindi Language/Hindi Typing/Hindi stenography training according to the roaster.\nPublication of In-House Magazine ‘Raksha Anusandhan Bharti’.\nPreparation of messages in Hindi for various in-House journals of various Laboratories/Establishments/Centres of DRDO.\nPreparation of consolidated Annual Reports of all Laboratories/Establishments/ Centres of DRDO for progressive use of Official Language Hindi in official work and sending the consolidated report to MoD.\nAttending Town Official Language Implementation Committee (TOLIC) meeting and follow up actions as per the directions taken in the meeting and meetings of the Hindi Advisory Committee Chaired under Hon’ble RM and follow up actions in accordance with decisions thereof.\nNotification of Laboratories/Establishments/Centres under Rule 10(4) of Rajbhasha Rules 1976.\nCreation of Hindi Posts and Hindi Cadre Review.\nTo carry out all O&M related functions in DRDO as mandated by Government of India.\nPolicies and Directives\nIn pursuance of constitutional and legal provisions of the Official Language Act, 1963 and Official Language Rules of 1976 and Orders, to promote the use of Hindi in the official work, the Directorate of Rajbhasha and O&M is responsible for implementation of Official Language Policy of Government of India in all DRDO Laboratories/Establishments and Corporate HQ as well as to carry out the required Organization & Methods (O&M) functions. Constitutional and Legal Provisions regarding ‘Official Language’ are as follows.\nPart 17 of the Constitution of India (Articles 343 to Article 351) makes elaborate provisions with the Official Language of the Republic of India. The main provisions dealing with the Official Language of the Union are embodied in Articles 343 and 344 of the Constitution of India.\nThe Official Language listed in the 8\nth\nSchedule of Constitution of India.\n1963 Section 3(3) Act of Official Language of Constitution of India.\nManual regarding the use of the Official Language Hindi.\n1976 Rules on Official Language made by the Deptt. of Rajbhasha under MHA.\nAnnual programme for transacting the Official work of the union in Hindi by Department of Official Language, Ministry of Home Affairs.\nReference\nwww.rajbhasha.gov.in\nImplementation\nIn persuasion of Act and Rules on Official Language, the Directorate of Rajbhasha and O&M circulate the Presidential orders and general orders, executive instructions/directions and the Annual Programme issued by Department of Official Language for transacting the official work of the Union in Hindi regarding Official Language policy, to all the Laboratories/Establishments of DRDO.\nPeriodically inspecting (as per prescribed targets in the Annual Programme of the Department of Official Language, monitoring and implementing all provisions of the Official Language Act, 1963, Official Language Resolutions dated 18 Jan 1968, Official Language Rules 1976, Presidential Orders issued from time to time and other regulatory directions (including Annual Programme issued by Department of Official Language every year) in all the Laboratories/Establishments of DRDO.\nTranslation work from English-to-English and vice-versa received from various Directorates of DRDO HQ.\nEvents of Directorate of Rajbhasha\nOfficial Language Implementation Committee (OLIC) Meeting (Quarterly)\nMOD (Ministry of Defence) Official Language Implementation Committee (Quarterly)\nHindi Workshop (Quarterly)\nHQrs Level Official Language Inspection (Minimum 25% of total Laboratories/Establishments in a year).\nHindi fortnight (Yearly)\nHindi Diwas (Yearly)\nBook Award Scheme of DRDO (Yearly)\nPublication of Hindi Magazine “Raksha Anusandhan Bharti” (Yearly)\nImplementation of Incentive Scheme of Original Hindi noting/Drafting (Yearly)\nTranslation of English/Hindi official documents received from various Directorates of DRDO HQ in Hindi/English.\nParticipation in Town Official Language Implementation Committee (TOLIC) meeting (Half yearly)\nOrganizing cluster wise joint technical Symposium/Seminar (08 Clusters)\nNomination for Hindi/Hindi Typing / Hindi stenography training (Half Yearly)\nTranslation and refresher Training of Translators/Assistant Director (OL)/Deputy Director (OL) /Joint Director (OL) at Central Translation Bureau.\nOrganising the Orientation programme for progressive use of Hindi in Laboratories/Establishments/Centres (Quarterly/Area wise)\nReports and Magazine\nReports\nQuarterly Progressive Report\nAnnual Assessment Report\nAnnual Report\nImportant Forms\nInspection Questionnaire (HQrs & Joint)\nParliamentary Inspection Questionnaire\nThe above forms are available on\nwww.rajbhasha.nic.in"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/area-of-work/directorate-quality-reliability-safety-dqrs", "content": "DQR&S is responsible for formulation of DRDO policies covering quality, reliability & safety and their implementation. The broad Roles and Functions are as follows:\nFormulate and implement general policy on Quality, Reliability, Software Quality, IV&V and act as a nodal point at corporate level for these functions.\nAssist DRDO Labs/Estts in evolving appropriate Quality Assurance/Reliability (hardware and software) plans suiting their specific needs and to act as a catalyst to ensure that these plans are properly implemented.\nFormulate standards and guidelines for software development, software quality and reliability.\nPromote Quality Reliability & Safety culture in DRDO and bring in organizational changes where required.\nConduct the audit levels IV & V of the software assets of the Labs and recommend on the adequacy of prior IV & V carried out.\nCarry out Quality & Reliability reviews and to assist in internal audit of various DRDO Labs/Estts/Centres.\nParticipate as a member in Test & Evaluation Committee and Failure Analysis Board for Major/Catastrophic failure of systems.\nCoordinate within and outside DRDO on all quality related activities including compliance to ISO-9000 certification requirements.\nGuide each cluster of Laboratories to generate common standards for materials, components and workmanship.\nEnsure formulation of safety policy, establishment of safety structure and promotion of safety culture in the organisation.\nOrganize seminars/workshops and short term courses to meet the training requirements quality, reliability and safety"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/area-of-work/dte-interaction-services-business-disb", "content": "Roles and Responsibilities\n1.  DISB is the single point of interface between DRDO and Army, Navy, Air Force, Coast Guard, HQ IDS and MoD (Acquisition) and provides technical inputs for capital procurement of the Services in the following:\na.    Capital Acquisition Proposals\nb.    Formulation of various acquisition policies and procedures and provide DRDO’s viewpoint\nc.    Formulation of Services Qualitative Requirements (SQRs)\nd.    Formulation of Defence Procurement Procedure (DPP)\ne.    ‘Make’ Proposals identified by users\n2.  This Directorate also facilitates in the design and development of systems by DRDO by conductingjoint reviewsat different levels with the three Services and Indian Coast Guard.Biannual Reviews with the Services are also organised,at the Apex level, to monitor the progress of projects and get decisions for progressing further.\n3.  DISB analyses the Long Term Integrated Perspective Plan (LTIPP) of the Services and provides appropriate inputs for synchronization with LTTPP of DRDO.\n4.  Identifies areas where DRDO needs to take up developments based on user requirements.\n5.  Facilitates induction of systems already developed by DRDO labs, wherever there is a user requirement.\n6.  Resolves issues and facilitates way-forward among Users, Production Agencies and DRDO labs.\n7.  Analysis of agenda, participation incategorisation Committee meetings to provide DRDO’s views on categorisation.\n8.  DISB co-ordinates with the labs for rendering necessary technical expertise of DRDO to various committees/ mechanisms,constituted by MoD/SHQs, responsible for contributing towards capability building of Armed Forces."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/area-of-work/extramural-research-intellectual-property-rights-er-ipr#top", "content": "Extramural Research (ER)\nResearch sponsored in academic institutions under the extramural research (ER) scheme focuses on research on phenomena or observations that are not understood, and that lack of understanding is recognized as an obstacle to scientific or technological progress in the broad topic area of relevance to military R&D. The knowledge-base so generated is embodied in high-quality technical manpower and in the new understandings, techniques and design-tools developed through the basic research funded. Such new knowledge can also provide pathways to significant advances in the mission-effectiveness of traditional military roles.\nCollaterally with the expanded knowledge-base created, a primary end-result of the research that is funded under the ER is a networked group of qualified people whose expertise resulting from the research can be drawn-upon to build an exploitable area of new technology that has potential military applicability.\nER supports the instrumentality of Memoranda of Collaboration (MoC) between DRDO Laboratories & Establishments and academia. These MoC invariably involve more than one DRDO establishments and cover explorations and investigations on a range of topics within a broad subject arena that generically cross-link the research activities of the collaborating Laboratories & Establishments and the research-disciplines of the selected collaborating academic institution. Such institutional cross-linking is organic, not episodic, and is made operational in an MoC through a management structure which includes in its standing arrangements representation of directors of the collaborating DRDO institutions.\nER also provides financial assistance for organizing conferences/seminars/workshops on the topics related to DRDO's activities.\nIntellectual Property Right (IPR)\nThe IPR Group, presently, provides total support to DRDO scientists in the task of legally protecting the innovations made by them apart from fulfilling its other assigned roles. The primary functions of the IPR Group, presently, can be enumerated in the following:\nFilling of IPR applications including Patents/Copyrights/Design, etc., in India and abroad on innovations made by DRDO Scientists for legal protection to generated proprietary knowledge.\nPromotion of IPR culture in DRDO through conduct of ‘In-house’ Awareness Programmes, Workshops, Interactions, etc. and also Interactions with experts from related agencies and departments.\nMaintaining IPR database, managing of DRDO IPR portfolio and handling oppositions/litigations related to IPRs.\nProviding security directive on Patent Applications referred to DRDO, by Controller of Patents under Section 35 of Indian Patent Act.\nDrafting/Vetting of provisions on protection of IPR in agreements and MOU’s entered into by labs/cluster/organisation on collaborative R&D.\nEvolving DRDO response and other related inputs on various national level IPR issues and on draft Acts, International Conventions and Treaties on the subject.\nProviding active support to DRDO labs/clusters in preparation of IPR applications, patents landscaping and freedom to operate opinions.\nThrust Area/Technology Gaps\nDirected Energy Weapons\nCoherent Beam Combining of Lasers\nHigh Power Lasers\nMicrowaves\nParticle Beams\nStealth\nMaterial (Base, Paints, Adhesives)\nRedesign (Fuselage, Wings, Flaps, Cockpit)\nMultistatic Radars\nSchleiren Signature\nElectronics\nFaster Processor\nOperating Software's\nWide Band Operations\nSensors & Actuators\nNext Generation Devices\nAntenna\nHigh Power Devices\nVLSI & MEMS\nFault Tolerance Computers\nTHz\nPhotonics\nNight Vision\nQuantum Computing\nSingle Photon Generation\nSingle Photon Detection\nPhotonic Interconnects\nQuantum Key Distribution (QKD)\nQuantum Communication\nSecurity\nCryptanalysis\nAlgorithms\nCyber Systems: Hardware & Software\nData Mining\nDecision Support\nList of DRDO Collaborated Academic Institutions\nIndian Institute of Technology (IIT) Kahargapur, West Bangal\nIndian Institute of Technology (IIT) Madras, Chennai, Tamil Nadu\nIndian Institute of Science (IISc) Bengaluru, Karnataka\nUniversity of Hyderabad, Hyderabad\nDefence Institute of Advanced Technology, DIAT Pune, Maharashtra\nJawaharlal Nehru Centre for Advanced Scientific Research (JNCASR), Bengaluru, Karnataka\nIndian Institute of Technology (IIT) Delhi, New Delhi\nCentre for Materials for Electronics Technology (C-MET), Hyderabad\nAnnamalai University, Chennai, Tamil Nadu\nAmrita University, Kollam, Kerala\nGuidelines-For-DRDO-Grants-in-Aid-Scheme.pdf\n(43 KB)"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/area-of-work/finance-and-materials-management", "content": "Formulation and implementation of policies for Material Management, updating procedures and policies for procurement as per GFR/latest policies issued by GoI/MoD/CVC/MoF.\nFormulation and review of Delegation of Financial powers of various authorities in DRDO.\nFormulation and issuance of policy for DRDO for undertaking deposit works and jointly funded projects with services and DPSUs.\nNodal center for Budget Management of DRDO, involving allocation of budget to DRDO Labs/Estt, expenditure monitoring and projections at various budgetary stages.\nNodal point for all actions related to C&AG Audit at DRDO Hqrs level and Follow up Management of Test audit of Labs/Estts.\nNodal agency for taking approvals for Demand Approval, Financial Sanction, DP Extension, CNC Participation, Regularization of the losses reported by Labs/Estts beyond the delegated powers of DGs.\nNodal center for bulk allocation of Foreign Exchange and Management of Interest Bearing Advances.\nNodal agency for interaction with CGDA and other external agencies with respect to Finance and Material Management.\nNodal center for implementation of procurement through various      e-portals i.e. CPP portal and GeM in DRDO.\nNodal center for Concluding Air Consolidation Agent (ACA) contract for Air Freight of Stores.\nParticipation in CNC meetings to ensure compliance of procurement procedures for the procurement cases beyond the delegated financial powers of DGs.\nIssue of Authorization Certificates to Labs/Estt for issue of Customs Duty and GST Concessional Certificates.\nOther associated activities like training to DRDO personnel, assisting arbitration, clarification on policy issues etc."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/area-of-work/human-resource-development#top", "content": "Policy Formulation\nTo conceptualize, formulate, draft and obtain approval of competent authority for the policies pertaining to recruitment, career progression and personnel management of entire manpower of DRDO spread in various cadre.\nCadre Management\nTo formulate, obtain approval and issue Recruitment Rules for all cadre and Encadred posts and review /amendments thereof.\nManpower Planning\nIn a fast changing environment and multi disciplinary requirement of DRDO dynamic and systematic manpower planning is essential. To ensure that the manpower planning is reflective of organizational requirements and in conformity to laid down guidelines following measures are taken on regular basis\nIntegration of manpower plan with technology plan.\nForecasting of quality and quantity of manpower requirement.\nAuthorization of manpower and vehicles among various DRDO laboratories, establishments and work centers.\nProcessing of the cases for sanction of additional manpower, creation of posts and vehicles for DRDO.\nManagement of schemes of research fellowships in DRDO.\nProcess cases for sanction and amendment of Regular Establishment RE of laboratories /establishments.\nTo consolidate projected requirements from various users for filling up posts by direct recruitment and release vacancies to RAC/CEPTAM.\nEnhancement of Quality of Human Resource\nTo improve the organizational effectiveness through overall development of its human resource following actions ensue\nTo initiate timely action for training /retraining /reorientation of existing manpower.\nTo formulate & implement the policies pertaining to continuous growth.\nDesign appropriate training modules for new and existing manpower.\nTo formulate /collate/coordinate/implement policies with respect to R&T scheme, PhD Scheme, Post Graduate training scheme of Army, Navy & Air force\nHandling arbitration and smooth implementation of court judgments\nMonitoring of court cases related to HR issues being defended by the lab/establishment."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/area-of-work/industry-interface-technology-management#top", "content": "Role\nTo support and promote Indian Industries with DRDO developed Technologies and enhance their technical capabilities to become globally competitive.\nResponsibilities\nIndustry Interface\nDRDO in collaboration with 1800+ industry partners has developed several systems, equipment, products and technologies for military and spin off applications. DIITM Interface with industry and industry chambers for productionisation and commercialization of DRDO developed systems/ technologies.\nTechnology Management\nDIITM is the nodal agency in DRDO to evolve policy frameworks and address all matters related to Transfer of Technology (ToT) and issue of licenses to industries both in India and abroad for DRDO developed systems and technologies. DIITM is also involved in Limited Series Production (LSP), technology acquisition through offsets and interface with DDP regarding NoC for exportsapplications of industries. Brief about same is given below:-\ni. Transfer of Technology\nDefence Research and Development Organisation (DRDO) is a defence R&D hub which develops defence technologies, systems/products that are required for Indian Armed Forces. DRDO develops defence technologies in the project mode. In addition, DRDO also undertakes/ supports various technologies & products whose development has been undertaken through Research Boards, Technology Development Fund (TDF), Extramural Research and grant-in-aid schemes.\nWhenever, the system has been trial evaluated/ accepted by Armed Forces, the technology is transferred to the Indian Industries to manufacture for production and supply. While transferring the technology to the Indian Industries, DRDO provides the relevant 'know-how' to the industries in the form of Technology Transfer Document (TTD) and hand holding support to absorb the technology. DRDO also provides the freedom to industries to carry out value addition to the base technology. This is a qualitative difference for the Industries when they acquire technology from the foreign OEMs wherein generally such value addition is prohibited at the receiver's end. All such modifications/ improvements happen at OEM's end and often a new licensing agreement is required to be entered into by the industry for the value added product.\nSome of the defence technologies developed by DRDO have good potential and utility for application in civilian market too. Such technologies are transferred to the industries with dual licensing rights for defence sector (MHA and other Govt organisations) and commercial market. Thus, ToT of DRDO systems/ technologies to Indian Industries contributes leads to national development through self-reliance and industrial growth.\nii. Limited Series Production (LSP)\nThe mechanism of LSP aims to establish the production line and resolve issues (if any) related to production of DRDO product before bulk production. As opposed to an R&D Project which is essentially an in-house activity financed by R&D Budget, LSP is funded by concerned User, is time bound. It may necessitate co-opting industry partners and transfer of technology to such entities.\niii. Technology Acquisition through Offsets provision by DAP/DPP\nDIITM handles all matters related to Technology Acquisition by DRDO through offsets. All Technology Acquisition proposals are forwarded by the Technical Manager to the DIITM, DRDO HQ. These proposals are assessed by the Technology Acquisition Committee. The Committee recommends technology acquisition based on viability of the proposal, technology implications, options for availability of the technology, range and depth of acquisition indicated, absorption capability in the country, status of IP before and after acquisition along with the assessment of cost of the technology.\niv. Export of Military Products\nDRDO is one of the stakeholders in assessing the applications received from Indian Industries requesting for No objection Certificate (NoC) for exports of Military products to friendly countries. For export of Military products based on DRDO technologies, DIITM carries out all the coordination and interface for ToT and customisation to suit the export requirements.\nv. Access of DRDO Test Facilities to Industries\nThe Labs of DRDO have state-of-art infrastructure including ‘Test Facilities’. It has been decided to provide access to these ‘Test Facilities’ on payment basis to Indian industries (Private/Govt/DPSUs/PSUs)engaged in Defence manufacturing (including under ‘Make in India’ initiative). Directorate of Industry Interface and Technology Management (DIITM), DRDO HQ is the central agency at corporate level for industry interface."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/area-of-work/information-technology-and-cyber-security", "content": "Areas of Work\nOperation, Maintenance and expansion of DRDO’s Intranet.\nOperation and maintenance of DRDO’s Intranet’s Unified Communication Services.\nDesign, development, operation and maintenance of Transactional Systems, Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP), Websites, Video Conference etc. on DRDO’s Intranet.\nCreation and Operation of Central Internet Access Gateway (CIAG) for DRDO to ensure secure and survivable Internet Service.\nOperation of DRDO Internet Mail Service.\nPoint of contact for National Knowledge Network (NKN) and related services.\nCoordination and providing IT services including Internet to all the Offices/Directorates of DRDO HQrs or offices located in DRDO Bhawan.\nEvolving Information Security Policy and its implementation across DRDO through various laboratories / establishments.\nIncreasing Information Security Policy awareness and training\nEnsuring compliance and assurance through audit of networks in Labs/Estts.\nCollaboration with Academia in the field of Cyber Security."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/area-of-work/international-cooperation", "content": "DRDO engages in Defence R&D areas of mutual interest with Friendly Foreign Countries\nDRDO participates in all the G-to-G meetings at MoD/MEA level\nDRDO conducts bilateral engagements in Defence R&D with USA, UK, Russia, Singapore and Republic of Korea\nCoordinates Workshops / Seminars / Conferences / Brain-Storming sessions in various topics of Defence R&D\nExplore possible engagements with Industry and Academia"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/area-of-work/management-services", "content": "Divisions and Activities\nDRDO\nDivision\nFunctions/Roles of Respective Division\nActivities\nDMS Sectt\nManagement Support to the Corporate HQrs/ Technology Clusters/ Labs/Estts\nResponsible for providing Corporate support to DRDO HQrs Clusters/ Labs on all issues related to General Management and Welfare activities including comprehensive back up support.\nManagement Services (MS-I)\nManagement of DRDO Apollo Hospital, Unions/ Associations, Motor Accident Cases, Court Cases, DRDO Aided Schools and Parliament Matters.\nMotor Accident Claims Tribunal (MACT)\nProvide all necessary assistance to the Labs/Estts of DRDO, as and when required, for the court cases related to accidents of the Government vehicles.\nProcess lab proposals for getting charged expenditure sanctioned from MOD for compensation awarded by MACT/Court.\nDRDO Aided Schools\nExamine and process the proposals of DRDO Aided Schools for funding. Obtain expenditure status from the schools and verify their statements.\nDRDO-Apollo Hospital\nCoordination for all issues related to DRDO-Apollo Hospital.\nUnions/Associations & Court Cases\nVerification of memberships of the Associations.\nProcess the requests for recognition of Unions and Associations for approval of the Competent Authority.\nAdministrative Approval\nProcess proposals received from Labs for administrative approvals related to:-\nEstablishing MI room in the labs.\nPrivate security covers in the labs.\nParliament Matters\nPrompt draft replies to the Parliament Questions for onward transmission to DPA\nManagement Services (MS-II)\nProcurement /Hiring of Services, Stores Inventory Management (PSIM) for DRDO HQrs\nAnnual budget estimates for DRDO HQrs.\nReview of expenditure status under various Heads.\nProcess for procurement of stationery, office/ IT/communication equipment for DRDO HQrs.\nFinalise the Annual Maintenance Contracts for all the office equipment of DRDO HQrs.\nFinancial sanction for Cash Awards/Misc/ Expenses for DRDO HQrs.\nMaintain the stock ledgers and conduct annual stock verification.\nCondemnation of unserviceable items/equipment.\nManagement Services (MS-III)\nAdmin approval & financial sanction for security services contracts of DRDO Labs/Estts (under financial power of DMS & DG (R&M)\nProcess the Security Services Contract under 5.2 (ii) of Delegation of Financial Powers dated 28 June 2018, of all DRDO Labs/Estt, which falls under the delegated powers of DMS & DG (R&M).\nManagement Services (MS-IV)\nManagement of Budget, Finance and Accounts (BF&A) & Bill Processing\nExercise financial powers delegated to DMS as per Government letter No DRDO/DFMM/FA/83226/M/01/1174/D(R&D) dated 28 June 2018 in respect of labs proposals beyond the powers of the lab Directors.\nManagement Services (MS-V)\nDomestic movement and related financial issues\nProcess proposals for sanctioning of move of non officials. Prepare and examine their TA/DA claims and process with CDA (R&D) for payment.\nProcess proposals, related to the following, of the Labs/Clusters/Corporate Directorates for Approval of the Competent Authority:\nAir travel of non-officials in Executive Class.\nRegularisation of air tickets purchased through un-authorized agents.\nTime bar sanction for shifting of family and personal on permanent transfer.\nTime bar sanction for admitting TA/DA claims.\nProcess the requirements for issue warrants (for self); family warrants/Form D, etc. to Service Personnel on the strength of DRDO HQrs, as per their entitlements.\nIssue movement order for temporary duty move and TA/DA requisitions for financial sanction.\nProcess cases for regularization of loss of vouchers (tickets, boarding pass, etc.) in respect of temporary duty move.\nManagement Services (MS-VI)\nForeign Deputation\nProcess the proposals for foreign deputations. Obtain approval of the Competent Authority and political clearance from MEA. Arrange for issue of the Government letter and issue travel voucher.\nProvide necessary support/assistance to the officer proceeding on foreign deputation for issue of official passport, stamping of visa, blocking and issue of tickets by the authorized agents etc.\nManagement Services (MS-VII)\nManagement of Services of Transportation/Accommodation at Development Enclave Guest House\nEnsure that the transport requirements are met.\nEnsure that the DRDO HQ fleet of vehicles is utilized and properly maintained.\nPeriodically constitute Regional Station Boards (zone wise) to consider pre-mature condemnation of vehicles, on the Regular Establishment (RE) of the labs/establishments.\nIssue financial sanction to the Labs/Units for purchase of new/replacement of downgraded vehicles, on the basis of authorization.\nBooking accommodation for officers visiting Delhi at DRDO Guest House at Development Enclave, on daily basis.\nManagement Services (MS-VIII)\nManagement of Communication & Postal Services\nProcess the proposals from the labs and the DRDO HQrs, for approval of the Competent Authority for\nResidential Telephones.\nNew EPABX Exchange etc.\nISD facility on office/residential telephones.\nOfficial telephones in case of new establishments and any additional requirements in old establishment.\nAct as a nodal point for receipt and dispatch of Dak for all the offices/Directorates of DRDO Bhawan and its redistribution to the concerned offices/Directorates.\nManagement Services (MS- IX)\nLabour Welfare Activities\nAct as a nodal point for implementation and providing interpretations of various provisions under the labour legislations to the labs/estts of DRDO.\nPeriodically review and update labour and welfare policies of DRDO covering policies to be implemented, both all the lab level (such as Death Relief Fund, Relief Fund, Retirement Fund, Medical Benefit Fund, Labour Welfare Fund, Welfare Committees, etc) and at the corporate level (Such as DRDO Benevolent Fund Trust Defence Civilian Medical Aid (DCMA) Fund etc).\nHandle all representations and demands of Trade Unions Associations and Federations, made to DRDO and MoD (including disposal of the representations)\nPerform control functions over the Labour Officer/AL WC/DLWC and assign the additional duties amongst them in consultation with Secretary, D (R&D). Examine their returns / reports and advise them whenever required.\nManagement Services (MS- X)\nCoordination & Human Resources\nFinalise the contract for hiring of various categories of skilled, semi-skilled and unskilled personnel required on short term basis, for smooth functioning of DRDO HQ.\nLiaise with Directorate of V&S and MoD Security H-Block for issue of temporary security passes for the hired contract personnel for entry to DRDO Bhawan.\nPerform the role of nodal point for making arrangement for conduct of various National Events/Conferences/Awards Distribution etc.\nManagement Services (MS- XI)\nLiaison, Infrastructure and Facility Management\nLiaise with CW & E for maintenance of auditorium and conference halls at DRDO Bhawan, DRDO Camp at Timarpur, NGO Hostel at Development Enclave and Development Enclave Guest House.\nFinalise the contract for security services and hygiene control of DRDO Bhawan complex."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/area-of-work/personnel#top", "content": "The Directorate of Personnel is mandated to manage the personnel, administrative and service matters of DRDO employees. The functional domain of DoP caters to a wide range of activities concerned with an employee joining DRDO and encompasses the entire service and post-retirement life cycle. DoP activities include all activities leading to appointments, regularization and confirmation, compassionate appointment, management of career progression, postings/transfers, assessment, promotions, DPCs, acquiring higher education, medical facilities, administrative approval for travelling abroad on personal visits, disciplinary/court cases/litigation matters, grievance redressal, conduct of Joint Consultative Machinery (JCM) meetings to resolve employees’ concerns and creating a harmonious working environment.\nDOP’s endeavour has been to simplify procedures/policies for ease of work and implementation.\nInformation For Pensioner\nSPARSH website (https://sparsh.defencepension.gov.in/)\nHandbook for pensioners (https://doppw.gov.in/)\n1.\nSPARSH FAQs (Final)\n2.\nSPARSH Help (Final)\n3.\nSPARSH Services (Final)\n4.\nSPARSH Video Links (Final)\nAchievements\nCPGRAMS 7.0 has been successfully implemented in DRDO from 15th Aug 2022.\nServices Interaction\nThe organization has Service Officers from all three wings of the armed forces. The Officers are posted on tenure basis. This Directorate coordinates with all three services for their posting and other administrative affairs. In addition to this, DoP is also the nodal agency for Permanently Seconded Service Officers in DRDO. DoP also caters to project-based appointments of Service Officers at various Labs/Estts.\nGeneral User Information\nDOP is the Nodal Officer of DRDO for CPGRAMS and Chairman of Grievances Redress Committee (GRC) at HQr. The GRC meeting is held monthly to redress the grievances received through CPGRAMS and DRDO from the employees and pensioners of DRDO.\nThe constitution of Grievance Redressal Constitution (GRC) which is recently revised is as follows\nAuthority\nPost\nDirector, Personnel\nChairman\nDirector, Management Services\nMember\nCE/Rep of DCW&E\nMember\nUS R&D as Rep of DD(R&D)\nMember\nRep from  SSPL\nMember\nRep from DFMM\nMember\nAddl Dir/DRDS-II\nStaff Grievances Officer /Member Secretary\nThe contact details of Chairman, GRC and Grievance Box is available at the Reception of DRDO Bhawan, New Delhi.\nDOP is the Nodal Officer of Deptt of Defence R&D for the following e-governance program of Govt. of India\nCentralized Public Grievances Redress And Monitoring System (CPGRAMS)\nACC (Appointment Committee of Cabinet) Vacancy Monitoring System (AVMS)\nAadhaar Enabaled Bio-metric Attendance System (AEBAS)\nLegal Information Monitoring Based System (LIMBS)\nANUBHAV"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/area-of-work/planning-coordination", "content": "Guidelines for project formulation, project management and all other related aspects.\nMaintain database in respect of all projects, participate in project reviews and process high value project proposals.\nCompile Periodic Project Progress Reports on major projects.\nPublish DRDO Annual Report & coordinate for MoD Annual Report.\nAct as a nodal point for Long Term Technology Perspective Plan (LTTPP) and Vision Document for the organization\nEstablish Corporate Review (CR) Policies & convene CRs.\nLiaise with all Ministries & Departments of GoI, on behalf of the Deptt. of Defence R&D.\nEvolve & convey organization/departmental response to Draft Policies, Legislations and Guidelines etc issued by other Ministries/Departments of GoI.\nUpdate Citizen’s Charter and nodal point for Citizen’s centric grievances.\nMatters related to Allocation of Business (AoB) Rules of DDR&D."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/area-of-work/public-interface", "content": "Image building of DRDO through exhibitions, media interactions, press release, social media campaigns, competitions, advertisements, publications and participation in republic day parade."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/headquarter-directorates/call-for-proposal/directorate-futuristic-technology-management-dftm#top", "content": "Advanced research areas in Additive Manufacturing (download PDF)\nTo foster cutting-edge research in academia, proposals are invited in advanced research areas such as Additive Manufacturing for directed research initiatives. These areas aim to drive innovation and strengthen collaboration between academic institutions and research organizations, paving the way for transformative advancements in technology.\nResearch Problems for DIA-CoEs (download PDF)\nProject proposals to address complex problem statements across multiple areas like signal processing, space technologies, communication materials, and aerospace structures for directed research. Through collaborative research, these projects seek to develop solutions that enhance national defense and contribute to global scientific progress. Proposals should focus on solving critical issues and pushing the boundaries of knowledge in these high-priority fields."},
