{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/technologies/terminal-ballistics-research-laboratory-tbrl", "content": "Generation of high energy electrical pulse power through explosive driven magnetic flux compression\nDetonation wave shaping & dynamic shock compression of materials\nShaped charges and EFP technologies for defeat of armoured and naval targets\nBaffle Ranges for small arm firing practice\nSmall arm ammunition for Low Intensity Conflict\nEnabling technologies for Electronic Fuzes\nPulse Detonation Systems"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/technologies/vehicles-research-and-development-establishment-vrde", "content": "1. Armoured Engineer Reconnaissance Vehicle (AERV)\nArmoured Engineer Reconnaissance Vehicle (AERV) is designed to meet the tactical and combat requirements of the military engineers for carrying out terrestrial and underwater survey in hostile terrains. AERV is based on the BMP- II vehicle with amphibious capability to cross water obstacles.\n2. Vehicles for Multi Barrel Rocket Launcher)\nVehicles for Multi Barrel Rocket Launcher - Loader cum Replenishment Vehicle (LCR) and Replenishment Vehicle (RV) are designed Vehicles for Pinaka Multi Barrel Rocket System (MBRS). Currently in service with Indian Army.\n3. Riot Control Vehicles\nRiot Control Vehicle (RCV) - 'VAJRA' & ‘Mini VAJRA’ is a non- armoured, 4x2/ 4x4 riot control vehicle suitable for law Enforcement duties and transportation of security forces along with their riot control gears to the affected areas. It is quite useful for mob control during the law violation situations. These vehicles are available in 03 variants i.e., small, medium and large with seating capacity up to 6 persons, 13 persons and 26 persons respectively.\n4. Heavy Recovery Vehicle\nThe Heavy Recovery Vehicle (HRV) is Designed, Developed & Test Evaluated for handling of article weighing up to 25 tons. The HRV is also capable of recovering Solo vehicles. Tractor-trailer trains of lengths even up to 31m and 110t of train weight. UATT of vehicle is completed successfully.\n5. CBRN Mini-UGV\nCBRN Mini- Unmanned Ground Vehicle (UGV) offers benefit in terms of personnel safety in CBRN (Chemical, Biological, Radiological & Nuclear) hazardous environment for the Armed/ security forces and facilitates dismounted recce operation in congested /building/ urban areas. It facilitates detection & remote monitoring of contamination, sample collection using Robotic Arm, Day/ night vision and digital marking of contaminated zones. UGV payload system is modular & can be customized for alternate roles as per User requirements.\n6. UMV and URV for MBT ARJUN\nUnit Maintenance Vehicle (UMV) & Unit Repair Vehicle (URV) have been developed by VRDE to carry out field maintenance and field light repairs to electrical and mechanical assemblies of MBT Arjun during operations.\nSalient Features:\nDeveloped on in service 8x8 HMV\nFully containerized, Two containers- Accoustically insulated Genset Container and Heat insulated Workshop container\nCommonality between UMV and URV on same equipment/ facility\n3 Phase Onboard indigenous Diesel Generator of 20 KVA\nOil and Gas charging system for Hydro-gas Suspension Unit\nCompressed Air and Pneumatic tools All types of oil filling and grease charging system.\nElectric & Gas welding/ cutting facility in URV\nMechanical & Electrical Hand Tools/ Power tools Special Maintenance Tools of MBT Arjun in UMV\nFacilities - Fuel Replenishment Unit, Washing Plant, Battery Charging / Gas Welding / Cutting..\n7. Armoured Amphibious Dozer (AAD)\nArmoured Amphibious Dozer (AAD) is a tracked, armoured, amphibious vehicle, combining cross country performance with an effective engineer capability. The vehicle is based on the BMP- II chassis. It has been designed as a highly mobile earthmover and supportive vehicle for use in forward combat areas.\nSalient Features:\nSemi Closed Loop Hydraulic System, Dual Control System\nDozer Bucket & Boom - Armoured Steel, Bucket capacity – 1.9 m3\n8 t constant speed Hydraulic Winch with 90 m wire rope\nTwo Nos. Hydrojet Assembly at rear\n8. Rotary Engine for Nishant & Panchi UAV\nA 55 hp “Rotary Engine” has been designed & developed by VRDE for Un-manned Aerial Vehicle (UAV). Rotary engine integrated with indigenous UAV was flown and the engine meets all the requirements of UAV. The engine is achieved the altitude of 3.72 km against the requirement 3.6 km. This is the first kind of engine in non-gas turbine category to be allowed with Airworthiness Certificate in India by CEMILAC.\n9. Multi-Purpose Decontamination System\nThe Multipurpose Decontamination system (MPDS) can be used for decontamination of vehicles, personnel, equipment & terrain during CBRN disaster situations. Salient Features: • Chemical delivery system with redundant pump. • Diesel fired boiler and control system for delivery of hot/cold water and steam at high pressure. • The system can be used for decontamination of Vehicles, Equipment and Personnel. The system can be carried in vehicles & helicopter\n10. Unmanned Ground Vehicle\nUGVs systems, based on wheeled vehicle platforms, developed and demonstrated for surveillance and NBC recce. The Tele-operated surveillance payload comprised of CCD cameras, Thermal Imager & LRF. The Tele-operated NBC Recce payload comprised of Recce instruments, sample collecting system and Picket firing system to cordon off contaminated zones."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/advanced-systems-laboratory-asl", "content": "ASL is working on the following core technologies required for design development of Missile systems.\nSolid Propulsion\nPropellant Processing\nDesign of Solid Rocket Motor\nTesting and validation of Rocket Motors\nThrust Vector Control Systems &Advanced Composites\nPolymer Composites\nNano Composites\nNDT Technologies Aerospace Mechanisms\nUltra low shock staging system\nSoft pay load release mechanisms\nAircraft spin recovery mechanisms\nLarge booster ignition systems\nAir crew escape system Design and Analysis\nConfiguration\nSystem Analysis and Guidance Design\nMission Studies & Analysis\nGuidance & Control"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/aerial-delivery-research-and-development-establishment-adrde", "content": "ADRDE is a Pioneer Lab Engaged in\nSystem design and development of Aerodynamic Decelerators\nParachute Aerodynamics\nAerospace Textiles & its engineering\nDesign and Development of Aerial Delivery Platform of up to 16T class.\nDesign and Development of Aircraft Arrester Barrier Systems using hydraulics (water twister technology)\nDesign and Development of Controlled Aerial Delivery System\nDesign and Development of Lighter-than-Air Systems including Floatation Systems"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/aeronautical-development-establishment-ade#top", "content": "Major Thrust Areas\nPilot less Target Aircraft\nUnmanned Air Vehicle\nEngineering and Pilot Training Simulator\nFlight Control System\nPilot Selection System\nAir Weapons\nCore Competency of the Lab\nAerodynamics\nConfiguration Design and Sizing\nAirfoil Design, Analysis and Testing\nAerodynamic Design and Analysis\nAerodynamic Stability and Control Derivatives\nAir Load Performance Estimation\nCFD Simulations\nWind tunnel Testing and Data Analysis\nPropeller Design and Analysis\nWeapon Carriage and Store Separation Studies\nPropulsion\nPropulsion system integration\nFuel system management\nAir intake design and analysis\nBooster integration\nFlight Control\nFly by wire control System\nAutonomous Flight Design\nFlight Control Computer Design\nSafety Critical Onboard Software Development\nSensor Suite for GNSS based ATOL\nDesign and Development of CEMILAC certified Digital EMA Actuators\nHILS System Design\nINS and GNSS testing facility using Three Axes Motion Simulator and Global Navigation Satellite Simulator\nVerification & Validation\nFlight Control Computer Design\nCockpit Display System Design\nPilot in Loop Simulation\nFlight Simulation\nReal Time Simulator for Design & Evaluation (Manned & Unmanned Aerial Vehicles)\nFull Mission Simulators for Pilot Training (Manned & Unmanned Aerial Vehicles)\nPilot Selection Systems for Armed Forces (Fighter, Transport and Helicopter)\nPayload\nGimbal Payload Assembly\nIntegration\nAir Vehicle Design\nFlight Testing\nSystems\nSystem Engineering\nSystem Design\nConfiguration Management\nFlight Test, Tele-Command and Tracking\nTele- Command Design\nTracking System Design\nTelemetry System Design\nGround Control Station\nOnboard Telemetry Transmitters & Receivers\nStructures\nAirframe Design and Analysis\nCG Management & Layout Design\nAero-Elastic Analysis\nStructural Testing\nMechanical Design of Actuators\nElectronic enclosures design\nPayload housing Design\nUndercarriage Systems Design\nThermal analysis of LRUs\nDigital mock-up of UAVs."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/armament-research-development-establishment-arde", "content": "Armament Research & Development Establishment (ARDE) is striving for high technology-based cost-effective and reliable armament and weapon systems through sustained efforts in research, design, development, productionisation and induction into Indian Armed Forces.\nThe progress of ARDE over more than six decades can be viewed as a journey from the \"know-what\" and \"know-how\" phase to the \"know-why\" phase of armament design and development The capability of ARDE is involved in research, development, prototyping, test and evaluation, and transfer of technology activities, including limited scale pilot-plant production of crucial items in the complex multi-disciplinary field of conventional armament technology.\nCore Competence of Armament Research & Development Establishment (ARDE) are: Weapon Systems; Aerodynamics and Ballistics; Air Delivered Munitions; Air Pilot Plant; Free Flight Artillery Rockets and Launcher Systems; Fuzes, Sensors and Safety Arming Devices; Ammunition; Piezo - Ceramics Development and Pilot Plant Production; Warheads for Missiles and Rockets; PGMS."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/centre-air-borne-system-cabs", "content": "CABS is the designated nodal agency for the design, development, integration, flight evaluation and delivery of complex airborne surveillance system for Indian Tri Services. Towards this, CABS has established capability and expertise in following key areas:"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/centre-artificial-intelligence-robotics-cair", "content": "The current thrust areas of CAIR are:-\nResearch and Development in the areas of:\na) Advanced Artificial Intelligence based Information Processing Systems\nb) Autonomy and Cognition for Unmanned and Robotic Systems\nc) State-of-the-art Net-Centric Tactical C3I Force Multiplier Systems\nd) Cyber, Information & Communication Security Systems\nCAIR works in the above diverse domains for delivering Force Multiplier solutions to Services to address emerging requirements in Information, AI and Robotics domains of Fifth Generation Warfare.\nQuality Policy\nCAIR is committed to research, development and delivery of systems, solutions and services that fully satisfy customers in the areas of Tactical Communication, Communication Security, Net Centric Warfare, Information Security, Artificial Intelligence and Robotics.\nCAIRs Quality Objectives are:\n1)      Complete projects within planned time and cost\n2)      Ensure customer participation throughout the product development\n3)      Research and Development of relevant technologies\n4)      Integration of in-house developed technologies for systems development\n5)      Leverage available technologies, COTS and open source solutions for  development of products\n6)      Nurture competence in large system integration\n7)      Develop synergic partnership with industry\n8)      Collaborate with academic institutions and research laboratories\n9)      Encourage innovation through empowerment of human resources\n10)    Improve skills and competence of personnel by continual training\n11)    Provide technical consultancy to customer\nCAIR was ISO 9001:2008 certified in 2009 &recertified in the year 2012. CAIR was appraised for CMMI ML 2 in 2014. Recently, CAIR received the ISO 9001:2015 certification."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/centre-fire-explosive-and-environment-safety-cfees", "content": "Explosive Safety\nExplosive safety regulation(STEC regulations).\nDesign, Testing and Evaluation of new generation of Explosive storage buildings (Igloos, URP, HPM, UG).\nHazard Classification of newly developed/imported ammunition/explosives.\nDisposal of Ammunition by Low Order Detonation.\nSiting of new explosive buildings in MoD establishments.\nEnforcing compliance of STEC regulations through explosive safety audit.\nAccident investigation related to storage of ammunition.\nMaster Ammunition Storage Plan.\nQuantitative Risk Assessment (QRA).\nEnvironment Safety\nEnvironment safety audit as per DRDO Safety Policy.\nTechnical Advice on matters of environment safety in MoD Establishments.\nR&D projects on development of environment friendly technologies for treatment/disposal of toxic and hazardous wastes / effluents.\nGuidance on contaminated site management through Bio-remediation.\nRisk Hazard Analysis & Disaster Risk Reduction.\nEnvironment Impact Assessment for MoD establishments.\nFire Safety\nProfessional fire training programmes for defence fire service personnel.\nConsultancy in fire safety and Fire Fighting to MoD establishments.\nFire Safety Audit of MoD establishments.\nInvestigation of Fire Accidents.\nDevelopment of Fire Detection and Suppression System for Armoured Fighting Vehicles\nDevelopment of Water Mist based fire suppression systems.\nDevelopment of Fire protective clothing & equipments.\nDevelopment of Halon alternatives & respective fire suppression system.\nHalon Banking and Management.\nTest & evaluation of fire suppression chemicals, foams, halons & halon alternatives, FR Fabric, fire appliances & equipment etc.\nSTEC Pamphlet No.\nTitle\nSTEC Pamphlet No. 1.\nQuantity Distance Regulations For Military Explosives\nSTEC Pamphlet No.2.\nClassified List of Military Explosives For Transport and Storage\nSTEC Pamphlet No.3.\nConstruction of Buildings and Traverses For Military Explosives\nSTEC Pamphlet No.4.\nA Manual on Safe working Practices in Explosive Areas\nSTEC Pamphlet No.5.\nQuantity Distance Regulations For Liquid Propellants\nSTEC Pamphlet No.6.\nRegulations on Fighting Fires in Government Explosives Establishments\nSTEC Pamphlet No.7.\nSafety For Electrical Installations and Apparatus For Building and Areas containing Military Explosives\nSTEC Pamphlet No.8.\nAir Conditioning and Humidity Control in Explosive Areas\nSTEC Pamphlet No.9.\nRules For Conveyance of Military Explosives by Rail\nSTEC Pamphlet No.10.\nRegulations for the Conveyance of Military Explosives and Ammunition by Road\nSTEC Pamphlet No.11.\nRegulations for Conveyance of Military Explosives by Service Aircraft\nSTEC Pamphlet No.12.\nSafety Conditions for use of vehicles and Mechanical Handling equipments in Explosives Areas.\nSTEC Pamphlet No.14.\nInstructions on Processing/ Obtaining Deviation Sanction\nSTEC Pamphlet No.15.\nGuidelines on Provision of Fire Protection facilities to Explosives Building\nSTEC Pamphlet No.16.\nHazards from Electromagnetic Radiation to Ammunition containing Electro-Explosives Devices\nSTEC Pamphlet No.17.\nProtection of Explosives Buildings Against Lightning\nSTEC Pamphlet No.18.\nDisposal of Waste Explosives and Ammunition by Burning/Demolition\nSTEC Pamphlet No.19.\nRegulations For Packaging of Military Explosives\nSTEC Pamphlet No.20.\nRegulations on the use of Freight containers For Transportation of Military Explosives\nSTEC Pamphlet No.21.\nDesign and Construction Manual For Blast Resistant Structures Using Laced Reinforced Concrete\nSTEC Pamphlet No.22.\nSpecification of Fire Damper For Air Conditioning Ducts in Explosives Buildings\nSTEC Pamphlet No.23.\nHazard from Non Ionising Radiation and Safe working Practices\nSTEC Pamphlet No.24.\nSafety Guidelines on “Breakdown of Ammunition”\nSTEC Pamphlet No.25.\nRegulations For the storage Explosives in the field."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/combat-vehicles-research-development-estt-cvrde", "content": "The core areas of R&D for Combat Vehicles Research & Development Establishment (CVRDE) involves\nDesign and Development of AFVs\nHull and Turret Structures\nEngine and Transmission  systems\nRunning Gear Systems\nVetronics\nIntegrated Fire Control System\nSystem Engineering\nIntegration and Performance Evaluation.\nDesign and Development of AFV Variants\nRepair and Recovery Vehicle\nSelf-propelled Artillery Vehicle\nArmoured Ambulance\nCarrier Command Post Tracked Vehicle\nBridge Layer Tank\nMissile carriers\nDesign and Development of LRUs for Combat Aircraft and Naval systems\nAircraft Mounted Accessory Gear Box (AMAGB)\nPower Take Off Shaft (PTO Shaft)\nBLDC Generator\nBearings\nLanding Gear\nHydraulic Filters\nDesign and development of vehicles for unmanned missions\nUnmanned Tracked Vehicles based on BMP platform\nUnmanned Earth Moving Vehicles"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/defence-bio-engineering-electro-medical-laboratory-debel", "content": "Defence Bioengineering and Electromedical Laboratory (DEBEL) is involved in the R&D of Aeromedical engineering and biomedical technologies for ground soldiers, Aerospace and underwater operation; Percutaneous, respiratory and ocular protection against CBRN agents; Protective clothing and gears for pilots, submariners and combat vehicle crew and for troops deployed in extreme environments; Respiratory management systems for fixed and rotary-wing aircraft."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/defence-electronics-application-laboratory-deal", "content": "Defence Electronics Applications Laboratory (DEAL) is working on communication and surveillance systems based on software and cognitive techniques, satellite phones/miniaturised satcom terminals for futuristic satellites, data links for AEW&C and UAVs, high data rate VLF receiver, static and mobile tropo communication systems, underwater laser communication technologies and MM wave fencing.\nCore competence of the DEAL are software-defined radios, LOS and BLOS (Satcom) Data Links for UAVs, AEWC&S platforms, satellite communication and network management systems, VLF communication systems, troposcatter communication systems, millimetre-wave and microwave communication systems, meteor burst communication systems, image intelligence environment, data fusion systems, 2D-3D visualization and change detection.\nDEAL also has a facility for reception, processing and dissemination of high-resolution satellite imagery. Project development activities are ably supported by facilities like Technical Information Center, CAD, Precision Workshop, MIC Lab, EMI/EMC Lab, Antenna Test Range, Environmental Test Facility & Quality Assurance, DEAL has been providing communication support to Indian Antarctic Expedition and during disaster/natural calamities"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/defence-electronics-research-laboratory-dlrl", "content": "Some of the important areas of work which the DLRL has established are : Antennas and Radomes, MW/mmW super components, WB and NB intercept/monitoring receivers high accuracy DF and frequency receivers, digital receivers, signal analysers and processors, radar fingerprinting systems, wideband digital RF memory (DRFM), microwave power modules, high power jammers, multi-beam jammers, ESM, ELINT and COMINT system for land-based vehicles, helicopter, airborne, shipborne, submarine platforms, unmanned air vehicle, aerostats and satellite, integrated EW Systems."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/defence-food-research-laboratory-dfrl#top", "content": "No active record(s) available currently for this section."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/defence-geoinformatics-research-establishment-dgre", "content": "Core Competence\nSnow and Terrain related Hazard Assessment, Forecasting, Mitigation & Engineering solutions\nMapping, Dynamics, Analysis and extraction of features for Snow and Terrain\nRemote Sensing and GIS techniques for geospatial mapping\nSnow and Terrain Mechanics & Material Characterization\nTrafficability analysis and sensor based solutions for combat engineering,\nSoft computing, Machine Learning, Image processing, and Pattern Recognition\nInstrumentation & Sensor Design for Geospatial research\nNumerical & Statistical Data Analysis TechniqMes\nGround truth collection for various applications\nIVlodeling & Simulation for snow and terrain research\nRole & Charter\nSpecific\nDevelopment of Technologies for Data Gathering, Forecasting, Monitoring, Control and Mitigation of Landslides & Avalanches\nResearch & Development in Geo-informatics and Hyper Spectral Systems\nData Dissemination\nDevelopment of Digital Atlas of Different Terrains\nGeneral\nTo create Necessary Infrastructure & Test Facilities for R&D and LSP, as applicable\nTo generate all the necessary documentation\nTo support upgrades throughout product Life Cycle\nTo utilise technologies development by other labs\nTo utilise the Spin-Off technologies\nTo support and work with Govt. Agencies, Academia and Industry\nTo undertake Life Assessment and Extension activities, wherever required."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/defence-institute-high-altitude-research-dihar", "content": "The thrust of Defence Institute of High Altitude Research (DIHAR) has been to do directed basic research for productivity enhancement of fresh vegetable and animal origin food; agro-animal disease investigation and control measures; exploitation of high altitude plant wealth for herbal products formulation; up-gradation of native livestock germplasm, semi-permafrost based conservation of elite plant germplasm; post-harvest technology for agro-horticultural crops; improvised hatching technology, nanomaterials based technology for improvement of crop & animal productivity, and extension activities to improve lab-to-land communication."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/defence-institute-physiology-allied-sciences-dipas", "content": "Defence Institute of Physiology & Allied Sciences (DIPAS) researches in the field of physiology, biochemistry and nutrition to improve the operational efficiency of soldiers with micro and macro environments.  Physiological adaptation to environmental stress and human engineering for an effective man-machine interface.\nThe prime Research Areas of DIPAS are: high and extreme altitude physiology; heat, cold and polar physiology; exercise physiology; neurophysiology; yoga and adaptogens for performance improvement; clinical and cellular biochemistry; anthropometric database of Indian soldiers; nutrition for armed forces; man-machine interface: ergonomics; occupation health and toxicology; herbal intervention – phytochemistry; immunomodulation and vaccine agents; genomics and proteomics: molecular markers; biomedical engineering and nanotechnology."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/defence-institute-psychological-research-dipr", "content": "The core area of research of the Defence Institute of Psychological Research (DIPR) is personnel selection research, organizational behaviour, the human factor.\nPersonnel selection: Design, development and evaluation of a comprehensive personnel selection system: Construction, standardization and validation of Intelligence, Aptitude and Personality assessment tools and techniques based on psychometric principles: Training of assessors in personnel selection using the above-mentioned techniques/tools: Validation and evaluation of the selection system and modification thereupon.\nOrganizational behaviour: Modules for enhancement of leadership effectiveness, motivation and morale; Training modules for training academies; Combat Stress and its management in field formations; Leadership training.\nThe human factor: Man-machine system; pilot selection system; aviation psychology; environmental psychology."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/defence-laboratory-dlj", "content": "Defence Laboratory, Jodhpur (DLJ) is involved in basic as well as applied research leading to the development of technologies and products for water, heat and terrain management for military applications. The laboratory is pursuing basic research in all the three thrust areas: Camouflage and low observable technologies; Nuclear radiation management and applications; Desert environment science and technology."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/defence-materials-and-stores-research-and-development-establishment-dmsrde", "content": "Defence Materials and Stores Research and Development Establishment (DMSRDE) carries out studies for understanding continuous and quantum effects occurring at macro, micro and nanoscale levels in various non-metallic materials. Some of the notable products of DMSRDE are a bulletproof jacket, blast protection suit, NBC suit, glacier clothing, ballistic helmet, boot anti-mine and other personal protection systems.\nDMSRDE has developed competence in following areas of work: Polymers, composite materials, elastomers, speciality paints and coatings, allied materials; fibres and fabrics, coatings;. Characterization and evaluation of polymers, polymeric composites, elastomers and adhesives, fibres and fabrics;, lubricants and greases."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/defence-metallurgical-research-laboratory-dmrl#top", "content": "Since its inception Defence Metallurgical Research Laboratory (DMRL) has developed and established several technologies in the area of metallurgy and materials science. An extensive infrastructure, offering world-class R&D facilities for research in metals and ceramics, has been acquired and installed over the last five decades.\nCore Competencies of DMRL are in product engineering, production support and performance analysis of metals, alloys and composite parts, Process development and surface engineering, Extractive metallurgy of Ti, Mg and rare-earth metals, Design and development of speciality alloys intermetallics, Ceramics and composites.\nThe principal areas of R&D of DMRL are in materials for application in armour, ammunition, aerospace, and naval. These include advanced magnetic materials, nanomaterials and smart materials, advanced materials processing technologies, surface engineering, advanced metal joining technologies, and mechanical behaviour of materials especially at high temperatures, high strain rates and at small length scales."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/defence-research-development-establishment-drde", "content": "Defence Research & Development Establishment (DRDE) is involved in the development of sensors for biological and chemical agents, Development of devices for physical protection and decontamination, Synthesis, evaluation and process development for toxicants and antidotes, Development of instrumental methods for the analysis of toxic chemicals. Toxicity studies in animal models for the safety evaluation of chemicals, Development of immuno-based detection systems, Expertise in recombinant DNA and hybridoma technology for detection of and prophylaxis against infectious agents, Development of vector control systems, Ecofriendly biodigester technology, Expertise in training for creating awareness against biological and chemical attack."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/defence-research-development-laboratory-drdl", "content": "DRDL is working on several technologies required for the Missiles and Strategic Systems including; Aerodynamics and Airframe Design, Computational Fluid Dynamics, Solid, Liquid, Ramjet and Scramjet Propulsion, Precision Fabrication, Systems Analysis, as well as the Command and Control systems for missile based weapon systems.\nThe major focus areas of DRDL include; Systems Engineering and configuration design, Aerodynamic characterization, hydrodynamic studies, computational fluid dynamics, computational combustion dynamics, structural design and analysis, prototype fabrication, structural testing, non-destructive evaluation, design and development of propulsion systems including the Solid, Liquid, RAMJET and SCRAMJET rocket motors, design and development of weapon system elements including the C4I systems."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/defence-research-laboratory-drl", "content": "Development of technologies & products to enhance combat efficiency of soldiers in jungle warfare.\nVector and vector-borne disease control management and technologies.\nWater quality assessment and development of products for amelioration of water quality\nHuman and organic waste management at high altitude\nExploration of Bio-resource for defence application"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/drdo-young-scientist-laboratory-artificial-intelligence-dysl-ai", "content": ""},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/drdo-young-scientist-laboratory-dysl-at", "content": "Weaponized UAV System\nDevelopment of Swarm Algorithms for ISR andCombat scenarios\nIntelligence gathering through various sensors\nIndigenous Flight controller Development"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/drdo-young-scientist-laboratory-dysl-ct", "content": "Accurate Radar Tracking with optimal resource utilization.\nNon-Co-operative Target Recognition and Classification.\nPassive Coherent Location Radar - Multi target Tracking and Association.\nTactical Spectrum sensing.\nOnline Waveform Adaptation.\nDeep Neural Networks.\nReinforcement Learning.\nCognitive surveillance."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/drdo-young-scientist-laboratory-dysl-qt", "content": "Design and development of superconducting principle-based Qubit-based Quantum Computing platform\nQuantum Algorithms for defence applications\nQuantum Random Number Generators for Cryptography and Quantum Key Distribution (QKD) applications\nQuantum Key Distribution (QKD)\nExploration of Quantum Sensing Technologies"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/drdo-young-scientist-laboratory-smart-materials-dysl-sm", "content": "CURRENT RESEARCH AREAS:\nAccelerated development of smart materials using material informatics\nSoft Robotics\nBio-mimetic flapping wing Micro Aerial Vehicle\nMetamaterials\nThermoelectric modules for infrared cloaking"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/electronics-radar-development-establishment-lrde", "content": "Products\nActive Phased Array Radars in VHF, UHF, L, S, C and X Band\nPassive Phased Array Radars\n3D Multi beam Surveillance Radars\nAirborne Radars for helicopters / UAVs / Transport &Fighter Aircraft\nMan Portable Light Weight Radars for Homeland Security\nLight Weight Surveillance Radars for High Altitude Operations\nImaging Radars for Airborne/ space\nborne Platforms\nGround Penetration and Through Wall Imaging Radars for Home\nland Security/Counter insurgency/Anti\nTerrorist operations\nGround based Radars for Coastal Surveillance and Sea Surface Surveillance Radars on Naval Platforms and harbour operations\nRadars for Ballistic MissileDefence\nNavigation Radars for Underwater Vessels\nRadars for Long/Medium Range Surveillance\nRadars for Surface to Air Missile Systems\nRadars for Weapon Location (against Shells/Mortars/Rockets)\nSynthetic Aperture Radars (SAR)\nPassive Coherent Location Radar\nMultistatic Radar\nDrone Detection radar\nSurface Wave Over\nThe\nHorizon Radar\nTechnologies\nMechanically Scanned Antennas\nReflector Antennas\nHorn Antennas for HPM Technologies\nSlotted Waveguide Array Antennas\nMicro\nstrip Array Antennas\nMulti Beam Antennas\nElectronically Scanned Antenna Arrays\nPassive Phased Arrays\nActive Phased Arrays\nDigital Active Phased Arrays\nMechanical Hardware\nAntenna Assembly / Sub assemblies\nPedestals\nPower Systems\nCooling Systems\nDG Sets, UPS & Power Distribution equipment\nAntenna Power Supplies\nExciter Receiver Modules\nTransmit / Receive modules\nTransmitters / Exciter\nReceivers\nHigh spectral purity sources and high dynamic range multi-channel receivers\nDigital Beam Forming\nMicrowave Beam Forming\nLarge size Antenna frames\nPallet Assemblies\nAlgorithms& Functionalities\nSignal Processing\nData Processing\nImage Processing\nMulti-sensor Tracking and Fusion\nRadar Networking and Data Utilization\nPlatform Motion Estimation & Compensation\nPlatform Stabilization\nRadar Computer/Resource Management\nTransponder Tracking\nIFF Tracking\nWeapon System\nWeapon Location & Fire Direction"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/gas-turbine-research-establishment-gtre", "content": "Conceptual design and performance prediction of gas turbine engines & sub-systems to meet the aero & marine requirements.\nReal-time simulation for performance verification and control system design.\nInternal CFD of turbo machinery.\nAerodynamic, Thermal & Structural Design.\nVibration and Rotor Dynamic Analysis.\nMaterials and Life Prediction.\nDesign, Development, Testing and Evaluation of\nFan and Compressor.\nCombustor.\nTurbine.\nAfterburner & Exhaust Nozzle.\nRotor support & Internal Flow system.\nAccessory Drive Gear Box.\nDigital Engine Control System\nDesign, Development, Testing & Evaluation of Hydro Mechanical Systems & Digital Electronic Controller.\nHardware-in-loop Simulation and Testing.\nDesign, Development and Testing of Advanced Data Acquisition System for Engine and Components Testing.\nGas Turbine Engine Instrumentation.\nAdvance Manufacturing and Quality Assurance.\nTesting to meet Certification of Engine & Sub-systems.\nDesign, Development and Testing of Control Laws.\nModeling and Simulation of Electro-hydraulic Servo Systems.\nAircraft-Engine Integration.\nShip-Engine Integration.\nDevelopment of Techniques for Fatigue Life Evaluation and Residual Life Assessment of Aero-engine Turbines."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/high-energy-materials-research-laboratory-hemrl", "content": "High Energy Materials Research Laboratory (HEMRL) is involved in basic and applied research of high energy materials including synthesis, characterization and evaluation. Development and evaluation of solid propellants for rockets and missiles; solid propellants and cartridge case technology for gun ammunition; high explosive compositions and thermobaric explosives; design and development of pyrotechnics; development of explosive reactive\narmour\nand design and establishment of pilot plant facilities for high energy materials. It is also involved in design, development and fabrication of a wide range of instrumentation / electronic\nequipments\nfor study and evaluation of high energy materials.HEMRL also carries out the design and development of pilot plants for synthesis\nand scale-up\nof high energy materials and related non-explosive chemicals.\nHEMRL also provides technical assistance, guidance or consultancy services to other institutions / organizations, on matters pertaining to high energy materials, their applications and production."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/institute-nuclear-medicine-allied-sciences-inmas", "content": "Institute of Nuclear Medicine and Allied Sciences (INMAS) has established the NMR, PET-Cyclotron facility for enhancing combat efficiency besides providing clinical research to the Armed forces. The institute also pursues a strong research programme with application in various stress-related disorders using NMR techniques including metabolomics, in vivo spectroscopy and functional MRI (fMRI). INMAS has established several non-invasive and innovative nuclear medicine methods. For soldiers working at high altitudes, a method for diagnosis, prevention and treatment of various ailments including cold injuries (frostbite) has been developed. The institute is now working in biological radioprotection (radiation countermeasures); management of thyroid disorders; nuclear and medical imaging; combat casualty management; CBRNe medical management."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/institute-systems-studies-analyses-issa", "content": "Area of Work\nModeling, Simulation, Systems Analysis for Defence Application\nSystems Evaluation Studies\nCombat Model Development\nComputer Science and Applications for Defence Modelling & Simulation Domain\nOperations Research and Decision Support Techniques\nResource Centre\nReports and Magazines\nAnnual report\nHindi Magazine\nTechnology News"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/instruments-research-development-establishment-irde", "content": "Instruments Research and Development Establishment (IRDE) core competence lie in the design and development in the fields of optical, electro-optical and optronic instrumentation for surveillance, reconnaissance, piloting, navigation, guidance, target identification and acquisition, designation, ranging and countermeasures under all weather conditions during day and night. Fire control systems and devices. Infra-red search and track systems. Stand-alone surveillance systems. Optical design, holography, optical image processing and photonic target recognition techniques, adaptive optics, integrated optics, micro-optics and nano-photonics, terahertz sources and imaging and stabilised payload."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/naval-materials-research-laboratory-nmrl#top", "content": "Naval Materials Research Laboratory (NMRL) has been working for providing technological solutions to Indian Navy concerning marine materials, corrosion protection and energy requirements. The focused R&D areas of NMRL are: Development of metallic, polymeric, ceramic and composite materials; sustainable energy technologies based on fuel cell and supercapacitors; hydrogen generation technology; materials for underwater acoustic stealth of submarines and surface warships; protection technologies for marine platforms and structures of Indian navy; functional ceramics and naval alloys; bio-remediation of marine environment; sustainable green technology for marine environment."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/naval-physical-oceanographic-laboratory-npol#top", "content": "The laboratory has developed hull-mounted sonars for ships and submarines, towed array sonars and airborne sonars.\nMajor thrust area research of NPOL are Transducers embedded systems, material science and material engineering, ocean science signal conditioning and processing, signal processing algorithms optoelectronics.\nThe Lab also owns a research vessel called the INS Sagardhwani."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/naval-science-technological-laboratory-nstl#top", "content": "Naval Science & Technological Laboratory (NSTL) works towards the design and development of underwater weapons, associated systems and structural design of naval platforms. Presently major thrust area of NSTL are: Torpedoes (Light Weight and Heavy Weight), Decoys (Expandable and Mobile), Mines (Ground, Moored, and Multi-influence), Fire Control Systems (ASW and FCS for Countermeasures), Autonomous Platforms (Underwater and Sea Surface Vehicles) and Naval Stealth Technologies."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/proof-experimental-establishment-pxe", "content": "Proof and Experimental Establishment (PXE) conducts instrumented design and developmental trials of armament stores, i.e., weapons, ammunition and their components by dynamic firing;. Ballistic evaluation of armour materials.; Technical evaluation of imported weapons and ammunition;. Carries out various out-turn proof of weapons, ammunition and their components and armour produced by Ordnance Factories, PSUs and trade firms. PXE also undertakes periodical depot check proof; investigation firing for failure analysis; undertake range specific projects/ tasks in aid of supporting test and evaluation. Analysis of ballistic data and design parameters. Modelling and simulation of the ballistic impact of armour. Radar cross-section (RCS) and radar absorbing material (RAM) performance simulation facility."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/research-centre-imarat-rci", "content": "Presently, RCI is pioneering R&D in the cutting edge technology areas and spearheading a number of technology development, mission mode and S&T projects. In addition, RCI is also leading the design and development of smart guided weapons with precision strike capabilities. RCI is a research and technology hub in avionics, particularly in Inertial Navigation, Imaging Infra-Red (IIR) and Radio Frequency (RF) Seekers & Systems, Sensors, Guidance & Control Systems, On-Board-Computers, Mission Software, Flight Instrumentation, Power Systems, Systems Integration, Flight hardware-in-Loop Simulation (HILS), Environmental and Electro-Magnetic Interference/ Compatibility (EMI/EMC/EMP/HERO) test facilities. RCI has achieved many firsts in India in these advanced areas."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/research-development-establishment-rdee", "content": "Research & Development Establishment (Engineers) (R&DE(Engrs)) has established a technology-base for design and development in the areas of military bridging equipment; Mine warfare equipment; Electro-hydraulic drives, Actuators and controls; Specialized shelters and equipment for field defence and NBC environment; Launch systems and associated ground system equipment for strategic and tactical systems for air, ground and sea-based applications; Customized electrical power sources for military applications; Robotics and unmanned systems for military applications; Composite structures; mems based sensors and actuators; High pressure pneumatics and pressure vessels; Specialized platforms for military applications."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/scientific-analysis-group-sag", "content": "Scientific Analysis Group (SAG) is working on advanced mathematical and statistical analysis & development of tools, signal processing; high-performance computing; information security; soft computing techniques. Group has established facilities for electronic probing, communication signal & protocol analysis."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/solid-state-physics-laboratory-sspl", "content": "Solid State Physics Laboratory (SSPL) is equipped with facilities for nano-fabrication, crystal growth and epitaxy, including electron beam lithography, focused ion beam, III-As/N/Sb and II-VI MBEs, III-N/As MOCVDs etc. In addition, a facility for microstructural, compositional, optical and electrical characterization of semiconductor materials and devices has been setup. The major work area of SSPL relates to: RF Devices/MMICs; optoelectronic devices; cryogenic technologies; advanced materials; sensors"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/terminal-ballistics-research-laboratory-tbrl", "content": "Performance of armour defeating projectiles and immunity profiles.\nStudies of ground shock, blast damage, fragmentation and lethality.\nPreparation of safety templates for various weapons.\nStudies of underwater detonics and pressure wave propagation.\nExplosive forming, cladding and welding.\nDevelopment, production, processing and characterisation of different high explosive compositions.\nImpact & penetration studies and characterization of materials at high strain rates.\nTechnology for generation of high energy electrical pulse power through explosive driven magnetic flux compression.\nBlast, Lethality and Fragmentation studies of warheads, shells and other ammunitions.\nCaptive flight testing of Bombs, Missiles and Airborne systems.\nBallistics evaluation of various protective system like body armour, vehicle armour and helmets against small arm ammunition.\nBlast, Lethality and Fragmentation studies of warheads, shells and other ammunitions.\nCaptive flight testing of Bombs, Missiles and Airborne systems.\nBallistics evaluation of various protective system like body armour, vehicle armour and helmets against small arm ammunition.\nDesign and development of Baffle Ranges, Warhead and Exploder for Torpedoes, Bund Blasting Devices, Multi-mode Hand Grenade, Non-lethal plastic and frangible bullets, High voltage- high energy electrical power packs."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/labs-establishment/work-sphere/vehicles-research-and-development-establishment-vrde", "content": ""},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/laser-ordnance-disposal-system-lords#top", "content": "Back to Listing\nLaser Ordnance Disposal System (LORDS)\nLabs\nTechnology Cluster\nElectronics and Communication Systems (ECS)\nIt is a Directed Energy Laser System for remote disposal of unexploded ordnances, surface laid mines, IEDs and other explosive threats located above ground and detected by the sighting sub-system from safe stand-off ranges of 30-250 meters. \"LORDS\" is a self contained system along with all its support system integrated on TATA-LSV vehicle for stand-alone operation. LORDS comprises of six major sub-systems - Laser Sub-Systems, Laser Optics Module (LOM), Laser Pointing Unit (LPU), Thermal Management System (TMS), Power Sub-Systems (PSS) and Control System (CS)"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/life-sciences-research-board/about-us#top", "content": "Format for submission of ‘Concept Note’ to LSRB\nFormat of New project proposal submission\nVision\nTo expand and deepen the knowledge base in the area of life sciences in order to harness and nurture the research talent in the academic institutions of the country to meet the national needs of Defence Services.\nMission\nTo catalyse the needed cross-fertilization of ideas and experiences between scientific community in technical fields to provide the required support to soldier in operations and the knowledge for integrating “human factors” to optimize his combat effectiveness.\nCharter\nThe Life Sciences Research Board (LSRB) of Defence Research & Development Organisation (DRDO) was set up to support and strengthen research base in the area of life sciences through projects under Grant-in-Aid Scheme to meet the national needs of Defence Services. The scheme is coordinated through five specialist panels:-\nLife Support & Biomedical Devices (LSBD)\nSoldier Health & Drug Development (SH&DD)\nPhysiology of Extreme Environment & Behavioural Sciences (PEE&BS)\nBiotechnology and Bioremediation (BTB)\nFood, Agriculture & Bioprospecting (FAB)"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/life-sciences-research-board/concept", "content": "DRDO\nSr. No.\nAppointment\nAppointee Institute/Organisation\n1\nChairman\nProf Ashutosh Sharma, President, INSA,  Institute Chair Professor, IIT Kanpur, and\nFormer Secretary to the Government of India, DST\n2\nCo-Chairman\nDirector General (Technology Management)\nO/o DG (TM),  DRDO, HQ\nDRDO Bhawan, New Delhi\n3\nCo-Chairman\nDirector General (Life Sciences)\nO/o DG (LS),  DRDO HQ\nDRDO Bhawan, New Delhi\n4\nMember\nSecretary, ICMR/Representative\nIndian Council of Medical Research (ICMR), PB No.4911, Ansari Nagar, New Delhi\n5\n-do-\nSecretary, ICAR/Representative\nIndian Council of Agricultural Sciences (ICAR), Krishi Bhawan, New Delhi\n6\n-do-\nAddl. Financial Advisor (SM) & JS, DRDO Bhawan, New Delhi\n7\n-do-\nDG, AFMS/Representative\nO/o DGAFMS, M-Block, New Delhi\n8\n-do-\nAddl. DGAFMS (MR, H & Trg) O/o DGAFMS, M-Block, New Delhi\n9\n-do-\nIFA (R&D),\nDRDO Bhawan,\nNew Delhi\n10\n-do-\nDirector, NII/Representative,\nNational Institute of Immunology (NII),\nNew Delhi\n11\n-do-\nDirector, CFTRI/Representative\nCentral Food Technological Research Institute (CFTRI), Mysore\n12\n-do-\nHead, Radiation Biology & Health Sciences Division,\nBhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC),  Bombay\n13\n-do-\nExternal Expert from Academia (02 Nos.)\n14\n-do-\nChairman, Spl Panel on “Physiology of Extreme Environment & Behavioral Sciences (PEE&BS)”\n15\n-do-\nChairman,  Spl Panel on “Biotechnology and Bioremediation (BTB) ”\n16\n-do-\nChairman, Spl Panel on “Soldier Health & Drug Development  (SH&DD) ”\n17\n-do-\nChairman, Spl Panel on “Life Support & Biomedical Devices (LSBD) ”\n18\n-do-\nChairman, Spl Panel on “Food Science, Hill Agriculture, Bio-fuels & Bioresources  (FSH&ABB) ”\n19\nOne Director from any Life Sciences lab\n20\nMember Secretary\nMember Secretary, LSRB\nDRDO HQ, DRDO Bhawan, New Delhi\n21\n-do-\nChairman, Spl Panel on “Soldier Health & Drug Development  (SH&DD) ”\n22\n-do-\nChairman, Spl Panel on “Life Support & Biomedical Devices (LSBD)”\n23\n-do-\nChairman, Spl Panel on “Food Science, Hill Agriculture, Bio-fuels & Bioresources (FSH&ABB)”\nOne Director from any Life Sciences lab\n24\nMember Secretary\nMember Secretary, LSRB\nDRDO HQ, DRDO Bhawan, New Delhi"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/life-sciences-research-board/contact-us", "content": "Dr. Gaurav Mittal,\nMember Secretary,\nLIFE SCIENCES RESEARCH BOARD (LSRB)\nDefence Research & Development Organization\nMinistry of Defence\nRoom No. 409, 4rd Floor, DRDO HQrs Annexe,\nOld LASTEC Building, METCALFE HOUSE COMPLEX,\nDelhi-110054\nEmail ID\n: lsrb[dot]hqr[at]gov[dot]in"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/life-sciences-research-board/eligibility", "content": "Who may Submit\nGrants are offered to IITs, universities, colleges, departments, laboratories approved research Institutes, eminent scientists working in reputed R&D Organizations / Institutes with well established R&D facilities, Government and non-Government sector etc.\nWhere / Whom to Submit\nMember Secretary\n,\nLIFE SCIENCES RESEARCH BOARD (LSRB)\nDefence Research & Development Organization\nMinistry of Defence\nRoom No. 409, 4th Floor, DRDO HQrs Annexe,\nOld LASTEC Building, METCALFE HOUSE COMPLEX,\nDelhi-110054\nEmail ID\n: lsrb[dot]hqr[at]gov[dot]in"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/life-sciences-research-board/formats", "content": "List of Ongoing Projects\nDRDO\nS No\nProject Title\nPI & Institution Name\nLSRB Panel\n1\nHybrid Ocnis for food technology\nDr. K. Rajagopal, CFTRI, Mysore, Karnataka\nFAB\n2\nDevelopment and Characterization of second generation chimeric antigen receptor T-Cell tagged with riboflavin photo- Activation to specifically target adherent cancers, using patient derived HSCs Maintained under microgravity\nDr. Madhulika Dixit, IIT Madras, Chennai\nSH&DD\n3\nPhytotherapeutics mechanism against aflatoxin induced neurodegenerative disorders\nDr. Sadhna Srivastava, Jiwaji University, Gwalior, M.P.\nBTB\n4\nInvestigating role of serum magnesium in patho-physiology of heat illness and susceptibility among Indian population\nDr. Kalpana Kumari Barhwal, Bhubaneswar, Odisha\nPEE&BS\n5\nDevelopment of a scalable route for the manufacture of Prussian blue insoluble of pharmaceutical grade an FDA approved decorporation drug\nDr. Y V Madhavi, NIPER, Balanagar, Hyderabad\nSH&DD\n6\nDevelopment of new & novel radiation dosimetric OSL materials by ion implantation technique and chemical methods for improved dosimetry\nDr. Pratik Kumar, AIIMS, New Delhi\nSH&DD\n7\nEvaluation of locally available phytogenics as feed additives\nDr. Mohd. Tufail Banday, SKUAST-Kashmir, Srinagar, J&K\nFAB\n8\nDesign and development of natural hepato protective churnam for army personnel working in extreme conditions\nDr. Y Swarnalatha, Sathyabama University, Chennai\nFAB\n9\nStress related health problem management of college students by yoga with nutraceutical interventions\nDr. Dilip Kumar Nandi, Raja Narendra Lal Khan Womens College, West Bengal\nPEE&BS\n10\nCorrelation between psychological and physiological parameters of stress among staff and students an empirical study\nDr.Neeraj Kaushik,NIT, Kurukshetra, Haryana\nPEE&BS\n11\nHigh altitude-hypoxia mediates thrombosis in soldiers: inhibition of autophagy as a strategy to combat high altitude-low oxygen induced thrombosis\nDr. Rajdeep Chowdhury, Birla Institute of Technology and Science, Rajasthan\nPEE&BS\n12\nDevelopment of wearable kinesiological system using sensorized glove and existing motion capture system for uper limb kinematics and kinetics analysis during lifting tasks\nDr. Rajdeep Ojha, Christian Medical College, Vellore, Tamil Nadu\nLS&BD\n13\nDevelopment of thermally conductive elastomeric composites for liquid cooled garments to mitigate thermal stress\nDr. T Niranjan Prabhu, M.S. Ramaiah University of Applied Sciences, , Karnataka\nLS&BD\n14\nMesoporous antibacterial bioactive glass microsphere impregnatednon woven surgical cotton gauze based haemostatic dressing for profusely bleeding military wounds\nDr. Jui Chakraborty, CSIR- Cenrat Glass and Ceramic Research Institute,Kolkata,West Bengal\nSH&DD\n15\nA multilayered regenerative scaffold engineered to heal deep wound by mimicking anatomical architecture of natural tissue\nDr. Debrupa Lahiri, IIT, Roorkee, Uttarakhand\nSH&DD\n16\nScreening for drugs against the endoplasmic reticulum stress-responsive Rta proteins of Candida albicans for antifungal therapy: implications in wound infections\nDr. Sneh Lata Panwar, Jawaharlal Nehru University, New Delhi\nSH&DD\n17\nTargeting Redox-Regulated Signaling system for Radioprotection using Grape proanthocyanidins: Amolecular Approach for product development\nDr Krishna Das Saha, CSIR-Indian Institute of Chemical Biology , West Bengal\nSH&DD\n18\nDesign and development of affinity based sensors for the deectionof radiological compounds in point of CBRN emergencies using ZnO NPs functionalized by amidoxine and mugenic acid\nDr. Raj Mohan Balakrishnan, NIT-Surathal, Mangalore, Karnataka\nSH&DD\n20\n\"Non-Contact electrocardiogram system for continuous monitoring of cardiac system and to evaluate the armed forces personnel performance\"\"\"\nDr. N.R. Raajan, SASTRA Deemed to be University, Thanjavur, T.N.\nLS&BD\n21\n\"Development of a standard method for identification of dope dyed and exhaust dyed polyster fibers/fabrices\"\" \"\nDr. Prasanta Kumar Panda, The Bombay Textile Research Association, Ghatkopar (W), Mumbai\nLS&BD\n22\nDevelopment of Silver and Gold Nanoprobes for the optical and electrochemical monitoring of H2S, Cysteine and glutathione in human blood\nDr. Kaushal Kumar Upadhyay, Banaras Hindu University, Varanasi, U.P.\nPEE&BS\n23\n\"Development of rapid point-of-care diagnostic kit for infections associated with combat war wound and sepsis in armed forces\"\"\"\nDr. Ruchi Jain Dey, BITS Pilani, Talangana\nBTB\n24\nExploring Novel Biomarkers in Indian Pulmonary Arterial Hypertension Patients Through Transcriptomics Approach\nDr. Rashmi Rana, Sri Ganga Ram Hospital, New Delhi\nPEE&BS\n25\nDevelopment of AI Based predictive algorithms for bionic hand action recognitions\nDr. Govind R. Kadambi, M.S. Ramaiah University of Applied Sciences, Bengaluru, Karnataka\nLS&BD\n26\nNano fabric conditioner to impart antimicrobial property to nomex or high performance fabrics\nDr. G.S. Hikku, Chettinad Hospital & Research Institution, Tamil Nadu\nLS&BD\n27\nDesign Synthesis and evaluation of sugar conjugated pyridine oximes as reactivators of oganophosphorous nerve agent inhibited acetylcholinesterase\nDr Sabita Nayak, Ravenshaw University, Cuttack, Orrisa\nBTB\n28\nDevelopment of optoelectronic biochemical sensor based on calyx systems for the rapid detection of warfare agents\nDr V.K. Jain, Gujarat University, Ahmedabad\nBTB\n29\nHaemostatic Microcapsules\nDr. SomenathGanguly, IIT, Kharagpur\nSH&DD\n30\nProbiotics mediated host bioenergetic health modulation as a novel approach to the management of microbial infections\nDr. Vikram Saini, AIIMS, New Delhi\nSH&DD\n31\nAutomated Cognitive metrics classification framework to improve himan machine collaboration\nDr. Partha Pratim Roy, IIT Roorkee, Uttarakhand\nSH&DD\n32\nHuman performance enhancement via tDCS in VR and performance forecasting via machine learning methods\nDr. Varun Dutt, IIT Mandi, Himachal Pradesh\nSH&DD\n33\nDetection and Management of Pain: Pain to Pleasure by neuromodulation\nProf. SanjitDey, University of Calcutta, Kolkata\nSH&DD\n34\nDevelopment Alginate-Silver Nano-composite embedded with Adipose-Derived Stem Cells for the Healing of Deep Burn Wounds\nDr. Everette Jacob Remingtin Nelson, Vellor Institute of Technology, Tamil Nadu\nSH&DD\n35\nIntelligent Video Based Human Activity Analysis\nDr. Sunita Virant Dhavale, DIAT, Pune\nDIPAS\n36\nDesign and Development of an advanced 3D woven honeycomb composite structure shell with high energy absorbent liner for air crew helmet\nDr. Bijoya Kumar Behera, IIT, Delhi\nLS&BD\n37\nProductization and clinical evaluation of Bio-potential signal analysis system for mobility assistance\nDr M. Sasikala, Anna University, Chennai\nLS&BD\n38\nAssessment of speech, communication, oral motor function and its clinical determinants among the patients with Post Burn facial and neck contractures\nMrs. Sindhusha Chandran, JIPMER, Pondicherry\nSH&DD\n39\nIISc-DRDO-INMAS Collaboration to explore biomedical applications of shockwaves\nProf. Dipshikha Chakravortty, Indian Institute of Science, Bangalore, Karnataka\nSH&DD\n40\nMolecular Investigation in Renin Angiotensin Aldosterone System (RAAS) in Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) and Hypertension\nDr. Bhuvnesh Kumar, Sharda University, Greater Noida, U.P.\nPEE&BS\n41\nDesign and Development of smart nanomaterial-based biosensors for pesticide detection in food\nDr. PratibhaKumari, Deshbandhu College, University of Delhi, New Delhi\nFAB\n42\nTesting On-Spot Food Quality for the Defense Personnel and Combating the Counterfeiters using Simple Fluorophores\nDr. Manab Chakravarty, BITS Pilani, Hyderabad\nFAB\n43\nHand Held Electrochemical Platform for biomedical applications with particular emphasis on stress marker detection\nDr. A. Subbiah, CSIR- Central Electrochemical Research Institute (CECRI), Tamil Nadu\nLS&BD\n44\nUnderstanding the Molecular Events Involved in Cellular Responses to Heat Stress\nDr. Ashu Bhan Tiku, Jawaharlal Nehru Unviersity, New Delhi\nPEE&BS\n45\nDevelopment of RASAR - based Expert Systems for Ligand-Based Virtual Screening of Drug Like Substances against Various Toxicity Endpoints in a Non-animal Alternative Approach\nProf. Kunal Roy, Jadavpur University, Kolkata\nSH&DD\n46\nSynthesis and Biological Evaluation of New Clickable Ethynylestradiol-Folic acid based Dual Targeting 68GA radiolabeled PET Probes for Breast Cancer Imaging\nDr. Jasleen Kaur, Amity University, Uttar Pradesh\nSH&DD\n47\nDevelopment of reusable macrocyclic probes for detedction and separation of radioactive metal ions\nDr. Satish Kumar, St. Stephen's College (University of Delhi), New Delhi\nSH&DD\n48\nIdentification of key regulators and their controlling mechanism in a combinatorial Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis Network: An Integrated Bioinformatics Analysis\nDr. Shazia Haider, Jaypee Institute of information Technology, Noida Uttar Pradesh\nSH&DD"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/life-sciences-research-board/institutions-interacted", "content": "Indian Institutes of Technology, Delhi.\nIndian Institutes of Technology, Guwahati.\nIndian Institutes of Technology, Kharagpur.\nIndian Institute of Technology, Banaras Hindu University, Varanasi.\nUniversity of Delhi, Delhi\nUniversity of Hyderabad, Hyderabad\nUniversity of Allahabad, U.P.\nTezpur University, Assam\nJiwaji University, Gwalior, M.P.\nGuru Jambheshwar Univ. Of Sci. & Tech. Hisar, Haryana\nAnna University, Chennai\nJSS College of Arts, Commerce and Science, Mysuru, Karnataka\nICAR-Indian Institute of Agricultural Research, New Delhi\nICAR-Central Institute of Post Harvest Engineering & Technology, Ludhiana\nCSIR- Institute of Genomics & Integrative Biology, New Delhi\nCSIR-Central Glass and Ceramic Reserach Institute, Kolkata\nCSIR-Indian Institute of Chemical Biology, Kolkata\nNational Institute of Mental Health and Neurosciences (NIMHANS), Bangalore\nIndian Institute of Science (IISc), Bangalore\nAll India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS), New Delhi\nAll India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS), Bhubaneswar\nRavishankar Shukla Univ., Chhattisgarh\nIndian Institute of Crop Processing Technology, Tamil Nadu\nCentre of Biomedical Research, Lucknow"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/life-sciences-research-board/norms-tot", "content": "Biotechnology & Bioremediation (BTB) Panel\nPanel Chairman :\nDr. Manmohan Parida, Director, DRDE, Gwalior\nPanel Members :\nDr. J. K. Batra, NII, New Delhi\nDr. R.P. Singh, IIT Roorkee\nDr. G.B.K.S. Prasad, Jiwaji Univ., Gwalior\nDr. Anil Kumar Gaur, GBPUAT, Uttarakhand\nDirector / Rep. from INMAS\nDirector / Rep. from DIPAS\nSpl Panel Member Secretary (Scientist),  DRDE, Gwalior\nPhysiology of Extreme Environment & Behavioral Sciences (PEE&BS) Panel\nPanel Chairman :\nDr. Rajeev Varshney, Director, DIPAS, Delhi\nPanel Members :\nDr. Rana Pratap Singh, Professor of Cancer Biology, School of Life Sciences, Jawaharlal Nehru University, New Delhi\nDr. Anita Kamra Verma, Professor, Nanobiotechnology Lab, Department of Zoology, Kirori Mal College, University of Delhi, Delhi\nDr. Latika Mohan,Professor of Physiology, AIIMS, Rishikesh, Uttarakhand\nDr. Meenakshi Chaswal, Professor, ABVIMS & RML Hospital, Delhi\nDirector / Rep. from INMAS\nDirector / Rep. from DIPR\nMember Secretary, LSRB/Rep\nSpl Panel Member Secretary (Scientist), DIPAS, Delhi\nLife Support & Biomedical Devices (LSBD) Panel\nPanel Chairman :\nDr. R Indu Shekhar, Director, DEBEL, Bangalore\nPanel Members :\nProf. N.R. Jagannathan, AIIMS, New Delhi\nDr. G. Ravindran, Anna Univ., Chennai\nDr. Rama Subba Reddy, IIT Madras\nProf. D. Sukumar, Sri Jayachamrajendra College of Engineering, Mysore\nDirector / Rep. from DRDE\nA senior scientist at the level of Joint Director\nSpl Panel Member Secretary (Scientist), DEBEL, Bangalore\nSoldier Health & Drug Development (SH&DD) Panel\nPanel Chairman :\nDr. Sudhir Chandna , Director, INMAS, Delhi\nPanel Members :\nProf. R. K. Seth (Retd), Department of Zoology, Delhi University\nProf. Pawan K. Dhar, School of Biotechnology, JNU\nProf. Surender Singh, Dept. of Pharmacology, AIIMS, New Delhi\nDr. Rakesh Kumar Parashar, Dept. of Chemistry, Delhi University\nDr. Ritu Kulshreshtha, Professor, Vallabhbhai Patel Chest Institute, Delhi\nDirector / Rep. from DIPAS\nDirector / Rep. from DRDE\nMember Secretary, LSRB / Rep\nSpl Panel Member Secretary (Scientist), INMAS, Delhi\nFood, Agriculture & Bio-prospecting (FAB) Panel\nPanel Chairman :\nDr. D.V. Kamboj, Director, DRL, Tezpur\nPanel Members :\nDr. Debendra C. Baruah, Professor, Tezpur University, Assam\nDr. S.C. Deka, Professor , Tezpur University, Assam\nDr. B.N. Hazarika, Dean and Professor, College of Horticulture & Forestry, Central Agricultural University, Pasighat Arunachal Pradesh\nDr. R.K. Goyal, Director Research, Maharana Pratap Horticultural University, Karnal, Haryana\nDr. V.S. Chauhan, Chief Scientist, CFTRI, Mysore, Karnataka\nDirector / Rep. from DIHAR\nDirector / Rep. from DFRL\nDirector / Rep. from DIBER\nMember Secretary, LSRB / Rep\nSpl Panel Member Secretary (Scientist), DRL, Tezpur, Assam"},
