{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/next-generation-avionic-displays", "content": "Back to Listing\nNext Generation Avionic Displays\nLabs\nTechnology Cluster\nElectronics and Communication Systems (ECS)\nDARE has developed Multi-Function Displays (MFDs) for modern fighter aircraft. Displays of size 5”x5” & 6”x6” have been developed and are in production for Su-30MKI aircraft. Large format Smart MFDs of size 6”x8” & 12”x9” for new generation aircraft have also been developed by DARE."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/ngarm-phh#top", "content": "Back to Listing\nNGARM (PHH)\nLabs\nDefence Electronics Research Laboratory (DLRL)\nTechnology Cluster\nElectronics and Communication Systems (ECS)\nIt is an ESM based Passive Homing head for Next Generation Anti-Radiation Missile employing BLI based DF receiver."},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/nineties#top", "content": "Back to Listing\nThe Nineties\nLabs\nDefence Electronics Application Laboratory (DEAL)\nPropagation Studies\nPropagation studies at millimeter wave frequencies for space to earth path\nCommunication Systems\nMeteor Burst Communication System (MBC)\nUtilizes trails of meteors at height of 80-120 kms\nLow probability of interception & High nuclear survivability\nWire Guided Torpedo (WGT) Modem\nMeteor Burst Communication System (MBC)\nCommand and guidance data link\n26 Km range between ship & torpedo\nSatellite Communication\nX-band Satellite Communication System\nReliable and secure comm in battlefield and adverse condition\n4.5M semi Static/Road Transportable Terminal\n1.5M Jeep/Jonga mounted/Air Transportable Terminal\n1.0M man pack terminal\nRapid deployment in remote location\n3M and 6M road and air transportable SATCOM terminals at C band Code named ‘NARAD’\nDeployed for peacetime and actual warzone operations by Indian Army without undergoing any formal user trial\nFirst DEAL product to be showcased in the Republic Day Parade\nData Links\nDevelopment of Data Links for unmanned aerial vehicle\nAdvanced Digital Communication Techniques\nDirect sequence SSM technique for antijam margin of 18db\nDigital video bandwidth compression technique\nPrecise range information ( ± 7.5 mt)\nTracking accuracy of 2 milli-radians\n160 Kms range command and tracking system\nPart of DRDO contingent in Republic Day Parade 2007\nLimited series production completed\nMillimeter Wave systems\n94 & 35 GHz front ends\n3 channel monopulse receiver at Ka band\nSensor for smart munitions\nHelmet mounted communication system\nmm wave FPA Imager\nThree variants single channel,1x16 linear array, 8x8 FPA imager demonstrated\nConcealed weapon detection, camouflaged targets and imaging of vehicles under foggy weather were demonstrated, validating its all weather capabilities.\nSatellite Image Reception\nEstablished an Earth Station for reception of Image Data from IRS series of satellites\nSetup satellite data transmission link with users and other DRDO labs.\nImage Processing\nSoftware development for processing Images received from Remote Sensing Satellites\nImages\nThe Nineties"},
{"url": "https://www.drdo.gov.in/drdo/nishant", "content": "Back to Listing\nNISHANT\nLabs\nAeronautical Development Establishment (ADE)\nTechnology Cluster\nAeronautical Systems (Aero)\nNishant is a multi-mission Unmanned Aerial Vehicle launched using a Mobile Hydro pneumatic Launcher with Day/Night capability used for battlefield surveillance and reconnaissance, target tracking & localization, and artillery fire correction. A sophisticated image processing system is used for analyzing the images transmitted from the UAV. The air vehicle has autonomous flight capabilities and is controlled from a user friendly Ground Control Station. Nishant is a highly mobile, compact and easily deployable system. It is recovered with Aero Conical Parachute and impact attenuation system.  An onboard flight control and navigation system enables the aircraft to fly in autonomous Way Point Navigation mode. Nishant has been inducted in Indian Army.\nImages\nNISHANT\nNISHANT"},
